[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Module","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Module"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Result-Types","page":"API Reference","text":"Every result type defines structural ==, isequal, and hash, so results can be compared, deduplicated, and used as Dict/Set keys. == follows the field semantics (a NaN quality or z-score is not == to itself); use isequal to compare results that may carry NaN fields.","title":"Result Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Exceptions","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Exceptions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Detection-Algorithms","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Detection Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Continuous-Methods","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Continuous Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Discrete-Methods","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Discrete Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Multi-Pair-Methods","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Multi-Pair Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Utility-Functions","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Utility Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#CorePeriphery","page":"API Reference","text":"CorePeriphery\n\nA Julia module implementing various core-periphery detection algorithms for network analysis.\n\nCore-periphery structure is a mesoscale pattern in networks where nodes are divided into a densely interconnected core and a sparsely connected periphery.\n\nAlgorithms Implemented\n\nBorgatti-Everett continuous model (2000)\nBorgatti-Everett discrete model (2000)\nLip's fast discrete algorithm (2011)\nRombach's generalized model (2017)\nSpectral method (Cucuringu et al., 2016)\nRandom walker profiling (Rossa et al., 2013)\nMINRES/SVD for asymmetric networks (Boyd et al., 2010)\nMultiple core-periphery pairs (Kojaku & Masuda, 2017)\nSurprise-based detection (Jeude et al., 2019)\nLabel-switching algorithm (Yanchenko & Sengupta, 2025)\n\nReferences\n\nBorgatti, S.P., Everett, M.G. (2000). Models of core/periphery structures.\nLip, S.Z.W. (2011). A Fast Algorithm for the Discrete Core/Periphery Bipartitioning Problem.\nRombach, M.P., et al. (2017). Core-Periphery Structure in Networks (Revisited).\nCucuringu, M., et al. (2016). Detection of core-periphery structure using spectral methods.\nRossa, F.D., et al. (2013). Profiling core-periphery network structure by random walkers.\nBoyd, J.P., et al. (2010). Computing core/periphery structures and permutation tests.\nKojaku, S., Masuda, N. (2017). Finding multiple core-periphery pairs in networks.\nJeude, J., et al. (2019). Detecting Core-Periphery Structures by Surprise.\nYanchenko, K., Sengupta, S. (2025). A fast label-switching algorithm for core-periphery detection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.CPResult","page":"API Reference","text":"CPResult\n\nResult structure for core-periphery detection algorithms.\n\nFields\n\ncoreness::Vector{Float64}: Coreness score for each node (higher = more core-like). Isolates (nodes with zero strength) always score 0.\ncore_nodes::Vector{Int}: Indices of nodes classified as core. For the continuous detectors this is the split produced by their partition keyword (a median rule by default), not an estimate of the model; isolates are never core.\nperiphery_nodes::Vector{Int}: Indices of nodes classified as periphery\nquality::Float64: Quality score of the partition (algorithm-specific)\nalgorithm::String: Name of the algorithm used\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.CPResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.CPMultiResult","page":"API Reference","text":"CPMultiResult\n\nResult structure for multiple core-periphery pairs detection.\n\nFields\n\npair_labels::Vector{Int}: Pair assignment for each node (1, 2, ..., K). Isolates (nodes with zero strength) are not fitted and carry the label 0; consumers that group nodes by pair should treat 0 as \"no pair\".\ncoreness::Vector{Float64}: Binary coreness within each pair (0 for isolates)\nn_pairs::Int: Number of detected core-periphery pairs K among the non-isolated nodes\nquality::Float64: Quality score (Q^cp)\nalgorithm::String: Name of the algorithm used\npair_selection::Symbol: :objective or :penalized pair-count rule\ncandidate_pair_counts::Vector{Int}: Evaluated model sizes\ncandidate_qualities::Vector{Float64}: Raw best objective at each model size\nselection_scores::Vector{Float64}: Scores used to select the returned model\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.CPMultiResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.CPDirectedResult","page":"API Reference","text":"CPDirectedResult\n\nResult of directed core-periphery detection. out_coreness describes how strongly each node connects outward to core-like receivers, while in_coreness describes how strongly it receives connections from core-like senders. coreness is their arithmetic mean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.CPDirectedResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.CPSignificanceResult","page":"API Reference","text":"CPSignificanceResult\n\nMonte Carlo significance result for a detected core-periphery structure. pvalue uses the conservative plus-one estimator (1 + count(null_quality >= observed_quality)) / (n_samples + 1). null_diagnostics is a NullDiagnostics recording the preserved constraint and switching completion.\n\nFields\n\nobserved_quality::Float64: detector quality on the observed network\nnull_qualities::Vector{Float64}: detector quality on every null sample\npvalue::Float64: plus-one Monte Carlo p-value\nsignificant::Bool: pvalue <= alpha\nalpha::Float64: significance level used for significant\nnull_model::Symbol: null model that generated the samples\nnull_diagnostics::NullDiagnostics: sampler diagnostics\nnull_mean::Float64: mean of null_qualities\nnull_std::Float64: sample standard deviation of null_qualities (NaN with a single sample)\nzscore::Float64: (observed_quality - null_mean) / null_std, an effect size in null standard deviations; NaN when null_std is zero or undefined\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.CPSignificanceResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.NullDiagnostics","page":"API Reference","text":"NullDiagnostics\n\nPer-run diagnostics of the null-model sampler used by cp_significance.\n\nFields\n\nrequested_swaps::Int: Edge switches requested per configuration sample (0 for other null models)\naccepted_swaps::Vector{Int}: Accepted switches in each sample\nswap_attempts::Vector{Int}: Proposals made in each sample\nmax_swap_attempts::Int: Proposal budget per sample\ncomplete::Bool: Whether every sample completed the requested switches\npreserved::Symbol: Constraint preserved by the null model (:degree_sequence, :in_out_degree_sequences, :topology_and_weight_multiset, or :expected_density)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.NullDiagnostics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.StationaryDistributionResult","page":"API Reference","text":"StationaryDistributionResult\n\nStationary random-walk distribution and numerical convergence diagnostics used by directed Della Rossa profiling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.StationaryDistributionResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.RossaEnsembleResult","page":"API Reference","text":"RossaEnsembleResult\n\nUncertainty summary from repeated paper-mode Della Rossa profiles. Optional raw replicate coreness occupies O(n * n_runs) memory; set store_replicates=false to retain only summaries after fitting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.RossaEnsembleResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.ConvergenceError","page":"API Reference","text":"ConvergenceError(msg)\n\nException raised when an iterative or numerical procedure fails to reach the requested accuracy: an ARPACK eigensolve that does not converge, a stationary distribution solver that exhausts its iteration budget, or a configuration-null edge-switching chain that cannot complete the requested number of swaps under the swap_shortfall=:error policy. Input validation failures keep raising ArgumentError or DimensionMismatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.ConvergenceError"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.borgatti_everett_continuous","page":"API Reference","text":"borgatti_everett_continuous(A; max_iter=1000, tol=1e-6, step=0.05, init=nothing,\n                            directed=false, partition=:median,\n                            threshold=nothing, core_size=nothing)\n\nBorgatti-Everett continuous core-periphery model.\n\nFinds coreness vector c that maximizes correlation with ideal pattern Δ[i,j] = c[i]*c[j].\n\nArguments\n\nA: Adjacency matrix (n x n)\nmax_iter: Maximum iterations\ntol: Convergence tolerance\nstep: Coordinate-search resolution in (0, 1]\ninit: Initial coreness vector (optional)\ndirected: With false (default) A must be symmetric and the correlation runs over the strict upper triangle. With true, asymmetric input is accepted and the correlation runs over all off-diagonal ordered dyads, as UCINET does for directed sociometric data; symmetric input gives the same fit either way.\npartition: Core/periphery split rule applied to the coreness vector: :median (default; nodes strictly above the median are core), :none (all nodes periphery), :threshold (nodes strictly above threshold), or :core_size (the core_size highest-coreness nodes, ties broken by lower index)\nthreshold: Cutoff used by partition=:threshold\ncore_size: Number of core nodes used by partition=:core_size\n\nIsolates (nodes with zero strength) always receive coreness 0 and are never in core_nodes. A warning is emitted (once per session) when A has more than one connected component with at least two nodes.\n\nReturns\n\nCPResult with continuous coreness scores\n\nReference\n\nBorgatti, S.P., Everett, M.G. (2000). Models of core/periphery structures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.borgatti_everett_continuous"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.rombach_continuous","page":"API Reference","text":"rombach_continuous(A; alpha=0.5, beta=0.5, max_iter=1000, tol=1e-6,\n                   n_runs=10, rng=Random.default_rng(),\n                   partition=:beta, threshold=nothing, core_size=nothing)\n\nRombach's generalized continuous core-periphery model at a single point (alpha, beta) of the parameter plane (Rombach et al. 2017, §4.2).\n\nThe core vector C is a shuffle of the transition-function template C*(α, β) of eq. (4.9), normalised so that its entries sum to 1 as in the paper, chosen to maximise the core quality of eq. (4.10), R_{α,β} = Σ_{i,j} A[i,j] C[i] C[j] (the full double sum, so each undirected edge counts twice). The search uses greedy pair swaps and random restarts. quality is R_{α,β} of the returned vector, not a Pearson correlation; coreness is C (its entries sum to 1; multiply by sum(C*) to recover the raw template values, which lie in [0, 1]). For the parameter-averaged aggregate core score of eq. (4.11) see rombach_core_score.\n\nArguments\n\nA: Adjacency matrix (symmetric)\nalpha ∈ [0, 1]: Sharpness of the core-periphery boundary, i.e. the size of the score jump between the highest-scoring peripheral node and the lowest-scoring core node (alpha = 1 is a discrete classification)\nbeta ∈ [0, 1]: Fraction of nodes in the periphery: the first ⌊βN⌋ template ranks are peripheral and the remaining N - ⌊βN⌋ are core, so the core shrinks from N nodes at beta = 0 to none at beta = 1\nmax_iter: Maximum swap iterations per run\ntol: Minimum objective gain for a swap to be accepted\nn_runs: Number of random restarts\nrng: Random-number generator used for restart permutations\npartition: Core/periphery split rule applied to the coreness vector: :beta (default; the N - ⌊βN⌋ nodes holding the core ranks of the template, i.e. the core implied by beta), :median (nodes strictly above the median are core), :none (all nodes periphery), :threshold (nodes strictly above threshold), or :core_size (the core_size highest-coreness nodes, ties broken by lower index)\nthreshold: Cutoff used by partition=:threshold\ncore_size: Number of core nodes used by partition=:core_size\n\nIsolates (nodes with zero strength) are removed before fitting: the template is built for the N' non-isolated nodes, isolates receive coreness 0, and they are never in core_nodes. A warning is emitted (once per session) when A has more than one connected component with at least two nodes.\n\nReturns\n\nCPResult with continuous coreness scores\n\nReference\n\nRombach, M.P., et al. (2017). Core-Periphery Structure in Networks (Revisited).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.rombach_continuous"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.rombach_core_score","page":"API Reference","text":"rombach_core_score(A; alphas=0.1:0.1:1.0, betas=0.1:0.1:1.0, max_iter=1000,\n                   tol=1e-6, n_runs=3, rng=Random.default_rng(),\n                   partition=:median, threshold=nothing, core_size=nothing)\n\nAggregate core score of Rombach et al. (2017) eq. (4.11),\n\nCS(i) = Z Σ_γ C_i(γ) R_γ,    γ = (α, β) ∈ alphas × betas,\n\nwhere C(γ) is the optimal normalised shuffle of the eq. (4.9) template found by rombach_continuous at γ, R_γ is its core quality (eq. 4.10), and Z is chosen so that max_k CS(k) = 1. This is the paper's recommended deliverable: a continuous centrality-like coreness that averages over the parameter plane instead of fixing one (α, β). The paper averages over the grid α, β ∈ 0.01:0.01:1 (10,000 points); the default 10 × 10 grid keeps the cost at 100 optimisations and can be refined through alphas/betas.\n\nArguments\n\nA: Adjacency matrix (symmetric)\nalphas, betas: Grid values in [0, 1] (see rombach_continuous for their meaning; beta is the periphery fraction)\nmax_iter, tol, n_runs, rng: Optimiser settings passed to each (α, β) fit, in grid order (alphas outer, betas inner) so results are reproducible for a seeded rng\npartition, threshold, core_size: Split rule applied to the aggregate score, as in borgatti_everett_continuous; default :median\n\nIsolates receive score 0 and are never in core_nodes. The result's quality is the mean core quality R_γ over the grid; algorithm is \"Rombach aggregate core score\".\n\nReference\n\nRombach, M.P., et al. (2017). Core-Periphery Structure in Networks (Revisited).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.rombach_core_score"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.spectral_method","page":"API Reference","text":"spectral_method(A; beta=0.1)\n\nApply Cucuringu et al.'s LowRank-Core rank-two reconstruction followed by Find-Cut. The published method requires a simple undirected binary graph.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.spectral_method"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.random_walker_profiling","page":"API Reference","text":"random_walker_profiling(A; tie_break=:deterministic,\n                        rng=Random.default_rng(),\n                        stationary_method=:lazy,\n                        stationary_distribution=nothing,\n                        stationary_tol=1e-12,\n                        stationary_max_iter=100_000,\n                        partition=:median, threshold=nothing,\n                        core_size=nothing)\n\nCompute the Della Rossa persistence profile of an undirected connected or directed strongly connected network. The default deterministic mode replaces the paper's random choices with lower node index; tie_break=:paper restores the published stochastic selection rule using rng. The result quality is the paper's cp-centralization. Directed inputs accept stationary_method=:lazy, :linear, or :auto, or a validated stationary_distribution supplied by the caller; a stationary solver that does not converge raises ConvergenceError.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ntie_break: :deterministic (default) or the stochastic, paper-exact :paper\nrng: Random-number generator used when tie_break=:paper\nstationary_method: Directed solver, :lazy (default), :linear, or :auto\nstationary_distribution: Optional validated stationary probability vector\nstationary_tol: L1 convergence tolerance for the directed stationary distribution\nstationary_max_iter: Maximum stationary iterations for directed inputs\npartition: Core/periphery split rule applied to the coreness vector: :median (default; nodes strictly above the median are core), :none (all nodes periphery), :threshold (nodes strictly above threshold), or :core_size (the core_size highest-coreness nodes, ties broken by lower index)\nthreshold: Cutoff used by partition=:threshold\ncore_size: Number of core nodes used by partition=:core_size\nn_walks, walk_length: Deprecated legacy simulation keywords. They are accepted and ignored (profiling is analytic); passing them emits a deprecation warning (shown with --depwarn=yes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.random_walker_profiling"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.rossa_stationary_distribution","page":"API Reference","text":"rossa_stationary_distribution(A; method=:auto, tol=1e-12,\n                              max_iter=100_000)\n\nCompute the stationary distribution used by directed Della Rossa profiling. The returned StationaryDistributionResult includes the L1 balance residual, iteration count, and selected method. :linear is a robust dense constrained solve; :lazy uses stored-edge matrix-vector products and is suitable for large sparse graphs; :auto selects between them. Input must be strongly connected, directed or undirected, finite, nonnegative, and loop-free. A solver that does not reach tol raises ConvergenceError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.rossa_stationary_distribution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.rossa_profile_ensemble","page":"API Reference","text":"rossa_profile_ensemble(A; n_runs=100, rng=Random.default_rng(),\n                       interval=(0.025, 0.975), threaded=false,\n                       store_replicates=true,\n                       memory_limit_bytes=512 * 1024^2, kwargs...)\n\nFit repeated tie_break=:paper Rossa profiles and summarize node coreness, cp-centralization, rank stability relative to the ensemble mean, and the number of unique profiles. Child seeds are drawn before fitting, so fixed seeds produce the same result in serial and threaded execution. Remaining keywords are forwarded to random_walker_profiling; passing tie_break (the ensemble always uses :paper) raises ArgumentError, and rng seeds the ensemble rather than a single profile.\n\nThe working profile matrix requires 8 * n * n_runs bytes even when raw replicates are omitted from the returned compact result. memory_limit_bytes checks that allocation before fitting so large ensembles fail predictably instead of exhausting process memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.rossa_profile_ensemble"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.minres_svd","page":"API Reference","text":"minres_svd(A; max_iter=1000, tol=1e-6, partition=:median, threshold=nothing,\n           core_size=nothing)\n\nCompatibility projection of minres_svd_directed onto CPResult. Use minres_svd_directed to retain distinct out- and in-coreness vectors and the least-squares residual. Keywords are forwarded to minres_svd_directed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.minres_svd"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.minres_symmetric","page":"API Reference","text":"minres_symmetric(A; max_iter=10_000, tol=1e-8, damping=0.5,\n                 partition=:median, threshold=nothing, core_size=nothing)\n\nFit the symmetric off-diagonal rank-one MINRES model A[i,j] ≈ w[i]w[j]. Simultaneous damped fixed-point updates make the estimator equivariant under node permutation and independent of row/column orientation. The returned coreness is w normalized to [0,1]; quality is the fraction of squared off-diagonal adjacency weight explained by the fitted model.\n\nThis is a distinct estimand from minres_svd_directed, which fits separate sender and receiver factors.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmax_iter, tol, damping: Fixed-point iteration budget, convergence tolerance, and damping weight in (0, 1]\npartition: Core/periphery split rule applied to the coreness vector: :median (default; nodes strictly above the median are core), :none (all nodes periphery), :threshold (nodes strictly above threshold), or :core_size (the core_size highest-coreness nodes, ties broken by lower index)\nthreshold: Cutoff used by partition=:threshold\ncore_size: Number of core nodes used by partition=:core_size\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.minres_symmetric"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.minres_svd_directed","page":"API Reference","text":"minres_svd_directed(A; max_iter=1000, tol=1e-6,\n                    partition=:median, threshold=nothing, core_size=nothing)\n\nFit the directed rank-one model A[i,j] ≈ u[i]v[j] over off-diagonal dyads by paired alternating least-squares searches. Matrix-vector products and work vectors are reused across iterations, including for sparse matrices. The returned out- and in-coreness scores are independently normalized to [0, 1]; residual is the unscaled least-squares residual and quality is the fraction of squared adjacency weight explained by the fit.\n\nThe two possible update orders are transpose-dual and their balanced factors are averaged, making the fit equivariant under transposition (which swaps out- and in-coreness) and under applying the same node permutation to rows and columns.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmax_iter, tol: Alternating least-squares iteration budget and tolerance\npartition: Core/periphery split rule applied to the coreness vector: :median (default; nodes strictly above the median are core), :none (all nodes periphery), :threshold (nodes strictly above threshold), or :core_size (the core_size highest-coreness nodes, ties broken by lower index)\nthreshold: Cutoff used by partition=:threshold\ncore_size: Number of core nodes used by partition=:core_size\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.minres_svd_directed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.borgatti_everett_discrete","page":"API Reference","text":"borgatti_everett_discrete(A; max_iter=1000, init=nothing, directed=false,\n                          cp_block=:ones, n_runs=1, rng=Random.default_rng(),\n                          method=:greedy)\n\nBorgatti-Everett discrete core-periphery model.\n\nFinds a binary partition maximizing the Pearson correlation between A and the ideal discrete pattern by greedy single-node flipping.\n\nArguments\n\nA: Adjacency matrix\nmax_iter: Maximum sweeps per start\ninit: Initial binary assignment (optional; used by the first start)\ndirected: With false (default) A must be symmetric and the correlation runs over the strict upper triangle. With true, asymmetric input is accepted and the correlation runs over all off-diagonal ordered dyads (UCINET's convention for directed data); symmetric input gives the same fit either way.\ncp_block: Ideal pattern for the core-periphery blocks. :ones (default) is Borgatti-Everett eq. (2), Δ[i,j] = max(c[i], c[j]), in which core-periphery ties are expected. :missing is their eq. (4), the model they recommend and UCINET's categorical default: core-periphery dyads are treated as missing data, so only the core-core and periphery-periphery blocks are correlated. See ideal_cp_matrix.\nn_runs: Number of greedy starts. The first start is degree-based (or init); the others draw a random core of random size from rng. The best final correlation is returned. With n_runs=1 the fit is deterministic.\nrng: Random-number generator for the restarts\nmethod: :greedy (default) is the single-node flipping heuristic above. :exhaustive enumerates every core set of size 1:n-1 (Gray-code order, O(2^n · n) time) and returns a global optimum of the correlation, the first one met in enumeration order among ties; it is available for n ≤ 24 and ignores n_runs, rng, init, and max_iter. Greedy flipping, even with restarts, misses the optimum on a few percent of small graphs because the correlation surface has many local optima, so prefer :exhaustive for small sociograms.\n\nIsolates (nodes with zero strength) always receive coreness 0 and are never in core_nodes. A warning is emitted (once per session) when A has more than one connected component with at least two nodes.\n\nReturns\n\nCPResult with binary coreness (0 or 1); quality is the correlation under the requested cp_block and dyad set\n\nReference\n\nBorgatti, S.P., Everett, M.G. (2000). Models of core/periphery structures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.borgatti_everett_discrete"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.lip_discrete","page":"API Reference","text":"lip_discrete(A; max_iter=1000)\n\nLip's fast algorithm for discrete core-periphery bipartitioning.\n\nFor a binary, undirected network, this implements Lip's exact algorithm. Nodes are ordered by decreasing degree, and the loss (missing core-core edges plus observed periphery-periphery edges) is evaluated for every proper, nonempty degree-prefix core. The smallest core is returned when several sizes attain the same minimum.\n\nArguments\n\nA: Adjacency matrix\nmax_iter: Deprecated compatibility keyword; ignored because the algorithm is exact. Passing it emits a deprecation warning (shown with --depwarn=yes).\n\nReturns\n\nCPResult with binary partition\n\nReference\n\nLip, S.Z.W. (2011). A Fast Algorithm for the Discrete Core/Periphery Bipartitioning Problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.lip_discrete"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.surprise_cp","page":"API Reference","text":"surprise_cp(A; max_iter=100)\n\nGreedily maximize the exact bivariate-hypergeometric joint-tail surprise of de Jeude, Caldarelli and Squartini (2019, equation 4) for a simple, undirected binary graph. The returned quality is the negative log tail probability.\n\nThe tail is evaluated from the peak of the hypergeometric terms outwards and truncated once the geometrically bounded remainder is below double-precision resolution, so every candidate flip costs roughly the width of the hypergeometric peaks rather than the full O(m²) double sum. The optimizer is single-start greedy node flipping from a mean-degree split.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.surprise_cp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.label_switching_cp","page":"API Reference","text":"label_switching_cp(A; max_iter=100, rng=Random.default_rng(), n_runs=1,\n                   directed=false, cp_block=:ones)\n\nLabel-switching algorithm for core-periphery detection.\n\nUses greedy optimization of the discrete Pearson core quality with random processing orders and optional random restarts. It optimises the same objective as borgatti_everett_discrete and is the stronger optimiser of the two: the random sweep order and restarts escape more local optima.\n\nArguments\n\nA: Adjacency matrix\nmax_iter: Maximum iterations\nrng: Random number generator used for node ordering and random restarts\nn_runs: Number of starts (the first is degree-based)\ndirected: With false (default) A must be symmetric and the correlation runs over the strict upper triangle. With true, asymmetric input is accepted and the correlation runs over all off-diagonal ordered dyads; symmetric input gives the same fit either way.\ncp_block: :ones (default, Borgatti-Everett eq. 2) or :missing (Borgatti-Everett eq. 4, core-periphery dyads excluded from the correlation); see borgatti_everett_discrete and ideal_cp_matrix.\n\nIsolates (nodes with zero strength) always receive coreness 0 and are never in core_nodes. A warning is emitted (once per session) when A has more than one connected component with at least two nodes.\n\nReturns\n\nCPResult with binary coreness\n\nReference\n\nYanchenko, K., Sengupta, S. (2025). A fast label-switching algorithm for core-periphery detection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.label_switching_cp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.multiple_cp_pairs","page":"API Reference","text":"multiple_cp_pairs(A; null_model=:er, max_pairs=10, min_pair_size=2,\n                  max_iter=100, n_runs=10, pair_selection=:penalized,\n                  pair_penalty=0.5, rng=Random.default_rng())\n\nDetect multiple core-periphery pairs (Kojaku & Masuda 2017, 2018) by jointly switching each node's pair and binary core/periphery role.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nnull_model: :er (default) uses the Erdős–Rényi null of Kojaku & Masuda (2017) eq. (3), p = m / binom(n, 2); :configuration uses the degree-preserving null of Kojaku & Masuda (2018) eq. (13), P[i,j] = d[i] d[j] / (2m), implemented exactly (including the paper's i = j null term). Kojaku & Masuda (2018) recommend the configuration null: under the ER null any network with a heterogeneous degree distribution exhibits core-periphery pairs, because high-degree nodes are \"core\" relative to a uniform density even when the structure is fully explained by the degrees; the configuration null only rewards core-periphery structure beyond what the degree sequence implies. :er remains the default for continuity with 0.1 and with the 2017 paper, so pass null_model=:configuration (or use multiple_cp_pairs_config) for substantive analyses.\nmax_pairs: Largest number of pairs considered; any value from 1 to n is accepted (the effective cap is n ÷ min_pair_size).\nmin_pair_size: Smallest pair allowed.\nmax_iter, n_runs: Sweeps per run and number of random restarts; restarts cycle through 1:max_pairs initial pair counts.\npair_selection: Rule choosing the number of pairs among the best k-pair solutions found. :penalized (default) maximises the objective minus pair_penalty * (k - 1) * log(n) / n; :objective maximises the raw objective, which is nondecreasing in k in practice and therefore tends to over-partition (and to vary with the seed) on a single planted pair.\npair_penalty: Penalty strength for :penalized.\nrng: Random-number generator.\n\nIsolates (nodes with zero strength) are removed before fitting and reported with pair label 0 and coreness 0; n_pairs counts the pairs of the non-isolated nodes, whose labels are 1:n_pairs. The null densities are those of the non-isolated subnetwork. A warning is emitted (once per session) when A has more than one connected component with at least two nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.multiple_cp_pairs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.multiple_cp_pairs_config","page":"API Reference","text":"multiple_cp_pairs_config(A; kwargs...)\n\nConvenience wrapper for multiple_cp_pairs(A; null_model=:configuration), the degree-preserving null that Kojaku & Masuda (2018) recommend. All other keywords are forwarded to multiple_cp_pairs; passing null_model raises an ArgumentError because the wrapper fixes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.multiple_cp_pairs_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.adjacency_to_matrix","page":"API Reference","text":"adjacency_to_matrix(edges, n; directed=false, combine=nothing)\nadjacency_to_matrix(edges; directed=false, combine=nothing, sort_labels=false)\n\nConvert an edge list to a dense Matrix{Float64} adjacency matrix.\n\nEach edge is a tuple (i, j) (weight 1) or (i, j, weight). Self-loops, negative weights, and non-finite weights are rejected.\n\nWith n given, endpoints must be one-based integers in 1:n and the matrix is returned.\nWithout n, endpoints may be any node identifiers (strings, symbols, integers, ...). The method returns (A, labels), where labels[k] is the identifier of node k. Labels are listed in order of first appearance in edges (first endpoint before second), or sorted with sort_labels=true.\n\ndirected=false (default) stores every edge in both A[i, j] and A[j, i]; (i, j) and (j, i) then denote the same dyad. directed=true stores only A[i, j], so (i, j) and (j, i) are distinct dyads.\n\nA dyad that occurs more than once with the same weight is stored once. A dyad that occurs with conflicting weights raises an ArgumentError unless combine names how to resolve it: :sum, :max, :min, or :last (the last supplied weight wins). With combine=:sum, repeated identical weights are summed as well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.adjacency_to_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.block_densities","page":"API Reference","text":"block_densities(A, result)\nblock_densities(A, core_nodes)\n\nObserved densities of the four blocks of the adjacency matrix induced by a core/periphery partition: the standard reporting companion of a Borgatti-Everett fit (UCINET's density table). Returns a NamedTuple (core_core, core_periphery, periphery_core, periphery_periphery) whose entries are the mean off-diagonal value of each block (the density for binary input, the mean weight for weighted input). A block with no dyads has density NaN.\n\ncore_periphery averages A[i, j] over core i and periphery j, and periphery_core over periphery i and core j; the two coincide for symmetric A. result may be any result with core_nodes and periphery_nodes fields (CPResult, CPDirectedResult).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.block_densities"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.ideal_cp_matrix","page":"API Reference","text":"ideal_cp_matrix(c; discrete=false, cp_block=:ones)\n\nGenerate the ideal core-periphery pattern matrix for a given coreness vector.\n\nContinuous model (discrete=false): Δ[i,j] = c[i] * c[j].\nDiscrete model, cp_block=:ones: Δ[i,j] = max(c[i], c[j]), Borgatti and Everett (2000) eq. (2): core-periphery dyads are expected to be present.\nDiscrete model, cp_block=:missing: Borgatti and Everett (2000) eq. (4), the model they recommend. Δ[i,j] = 1 for core-core dyads, 0 for periphery-periphery dyads, and missing for core-periphery dyads, which are excluded from the correlation. Requires a binary c; the result is then a Matrix{Union{Missing,Float64}}.\n\nThe diagonal is always 0.\n\nArguments\n\nc: Coreness vector (any real element type, values in [0,1])\ndiscrete::Bool: If true, use the discrete ideal pattern\ncp_block::Symbol: :ones or :missing (discrete model only)\n\nReturns\n\nIdeal core-periphery matrix (Matrix{Float64}, or Matrix{Union{Missing,Float64}} for cp_block=:missing)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.ideal_cp_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.core_quality","page":"API Reference","text":"core_quality(A, c; discrete=false, directed=false, cp_block=:ones)\n\nCompute the Pearson correlation between adjacency values and the ideal core-periphery pattern (see ideal_cp_matrix) without materializing dyad vectors. Undirected inputs use the strict upper triangle; directed=true uses all ordered off-diagonal dyads. With discrete=true and cp_block=:missing (Borgatti-Everett eq. 4), only core-core and periphery-periphery dyads enter the correlation and c must be binary. Returns 0.0 when either pattern has zero variance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.core_quality"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.coreness_scores","page":"API Reference","text":"coreness_scores(result::CPResult)\n\nGet coreness scores from a CPResult.\n\nReturns\n\nVector of coreness scores (higher = more core-like)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.coreness_scores"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CorePeriphery.cp_significance","page":"API Reference","text":"cp_significance(A, detector; null_model=:configuration, n_samples=999,\n                alpha=0.05, rng=Random.default_rng(),\n                detector_kwargs=NamedTuple(), pass_rng=true, n_swaps=nothing,\n                max_swap_attempts=nothing, swap_shortfall=:error,\n                threaded=false, thread_schedule=:auto)\n\nTest whether a detector's observed quality exceeds qualities obtained from random graphs. detector must return a result with a finite quality field. The :configuration null uses undirected degree-preserving double-edge swaps; :directed_configuration preserves binary in- and out-degrees through directed arc switches; :weight_permutation preserves topology and the edge-weight multiset; and :er samples an undirected Erdős-Rényi graph with observed density.\n\nThe returned CPSignificanceResult carries the plus-one Monte Carlo pvalue together with effect sizes: null_mean, null_std (sample standard deviation, NaN for a single sample), and zscore, the observed quality minus the null mean in null standard deviations (NaN when the null distribution is degenerate).\n\nNull samples are drawn in the storage format of A: a SparseMatrixCSC input is sampled directly into compressed sparse column form, so detectors use their sparse kernels and memory stays O(workers · (n + m)) instead of O(workers · n²). Dense and sparse inputs of the same network give identical results for the same rng.\n\nConfiguration samples must complete all requested swaps by default; a shortfall raises ConvergenceError. Use max_swap_attempts to set the proposal budget and swap_shortfall=:warn or :accept to retain incomplete samples explicitly. Per-sample accepted swaps and attempt counts are returned in null_diagnostics (a NullDiagnostics).\n\nWith pass_rng=true (the default) a fresh child RNG, derived from rng, is passed as the rng keyword to every detector call whose method accepts one, so stochastic detectors produce reproducible null distributions. Detectors without an rng keyword are called without it; for detectors defined outside CorePeriphery a warning is emitted once, because their null distribution is then not reproducible from rng. Pass pass_rng=false to never forward it.\n\nWith threaded=true, null samples are distributed over Julia threads. Sample seeds are generated serially before execution and every worker owns its sampling workspace, so serial and threaded runs are bit-for-bit identical for deterministic detectors and for stochastic detectors receiving rng. Detectors must be thread-safe. thread_schedule=:auto (and :greedy) uses a work queue with one task per thread, which balances irregular detector runtimes; :static assigns samples round-robin to one task per thread and cannot be used from inside another threaded region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorePeriphery.cp_significance"},{"category":"section","location":"contracts/#Algorithm-and-Result-Contracts","page":"Contracts","text":"CorePeriphery.jl uses common result containers where useful, but the numerical meaning of a score or quality value remains algorithm-specific. In particular, raw quality values must not be used to rank different algorithms unless they optimize the same objective on the same scale.","title":"Algorithm and Result Contracts"},{"category":"section","location":"contracts/#Detector-contracts","page":"Contracts","text":"Detector Input contract coreness meaning Partition rule quality meaning Randomness Main computational cost\nborgatti_everett_continuous Nonnegative, loop-free, undirected weighted; asymmetric input with directed=true Optimized continuous product-pattern coordinate in [0, 1] partition keyword, default :median (strictly above the median) Pearson correlation with c*c' over undirected dyads (all ordered off-diagonal dyads when directed) None Cached coordinate sweeps\nborgatti_everett_discrete Nonnegative, loop-free, undirected weighted; asymmetric input with directed=true Binary discrete role Returned binary state Pearson correlation with the cp_block pattern: :ones is max(c_i,c_j) (BE eq. 2), :missing correlates only core-core and periphery-periphery dyads (BE eq. 4) None with n_runs=1; explicit RNG for restarts; method=:exhaustive (n ≤ 24) is a deterministic global optimum Greedy label sweeps, or O(2^n · n) Gray-code enumeration\nlip_discrete Simple undirected binary Binary degree-prefix role Exact minimizing prefix; smallest tied prefix Discrete Pearson correlation of the returned partition (the Lip loss is minimised but not returned) None Sort plus linear prefix scan, O(m+n log n)\nrombach_continuous Nonnegative, loop-free, undirected weighted Shuffle of the Rombach eq. (4.9) template C*(α, β) with ⌊βN⌋ peripheral ranks, normalised to sum to 1 partition keyword, default :beta (the N − ⌊βN⌋ core ranks of the template) Eq. (4.10) core quality R = Σ_{i,j} A_ij C_i C_j (full double sum) Explicit RNG across restarts Pair-swap sweeps over template permutations\nrombach_core_score Same as rombach_continuous Eq. (4.11) aggregate core score over the alphas × betas grid, scaled so the maximum is 1 partition keyword, default :median Mean R_γ over the grid Explicit RNG, consumed in grid order One rombach_continuous optimisation per grid point\nspectral_method Simple undirected binary Normalized thresholded rank-two reconstruction row score Published Find-Cut optimum, not a score threshold Core plus cross density minus periphery density None Two eigenpairs (LAPACK dense, ARPACK sparse) plus a thresholded reconstruction pruned to the rows that can exceed the threshold (quadratic in their number)\nrandom_walker_profiling Connected undirected or strongly connected directed, nonnegative, loop-free Persistence when the node enters the growing periphery partition keyword, default :median Della Rossa equation-(8) cp-centralization Node-index default or explicit paper RNG O(n^2) greedy scan plus directed stationary solve\nminres_symmetric Nonnegative, loop-free, undirected weighted Normalized symmetric rank-one factor partition keyword, default :median Fraction of squared off-diagonal weight explained by w*w' None Matrix-vector fixed-point iterations\nminres_svd_directed Nonnegative, loop-free, directed or undirected weighted Separate normalized sender, receiver, and mean factors partition keyword, default :median Fraction of squared off-diagonal weight explained by u*v' None Paired alternating least-squares matrix-vector iterations\nminres_svd Same as directed MINRES Compatibility projection of mean sender/receiver score Directed MINRES partition Directed MINRES explained squared weight None Same as minres_svd_directed\nmultiple_cp_pairs Nonnegative, loop-free, undirected weighted Binary role within an explicit pair (0 for isolates, which get pair label 0) Joint pair and role state; pair count chosen by pair_selection (default :penalized) Kojaku–Masuda (2017) eq. (3) / m under the ER null, or Kojaku–Masuda (2018) eq. (13) exactly under the configuration null Explicit RNG across restarts Local adjacency-list moves across pair counts\nmultiple_cp_pairs_config Same as multiple pairs Same Same Configuration-null specialization (eq. 13) Explicit RNG Same\nsurprise_cp Simple undirected binary Binary role Returned optimized state Negative log multivariate-hypergeometric joint-tail probability None Label sweeps with cached block counts; each tail evaluation costs roughly the product of the two hypergeometric peak widths\nlabel_switching_cp Nonnegative, loop-free, undirected weighted; asymmetric input with directed=true Binary role Returned optimized state Discrete Borgatti–Everett Pearson correlation under the chosen cp_block Explicit RNG and optional restarts Cached label-switching sweeps\n\nRow notes:\n\nrombach_continuous: beta is the fraction of nodes in the periphery (paper semantics): the first ⌊βN⌋ template ranks are peripheral, so the core shrinks from N nodes at beta = 0 to none at beta = 1. alpha sets the size of the score jump between the highest peripheral and lowest core rank. coreness sums to 1 (multiply by the template sum to recover raw [0, 1] template values). quality is the eq. (4.10) objective of one (α, β) point; it depends on the template as well as on the data, so do not select (α, β) by maximising it. The paper's deliverable is the grid-averaged rombach_core_score.\nlip_discrete: the optimiser minimises the published Lip loss, but the returned quality is the discrete Borgatti–Everett correlation of that partition, so it can be negative on inputs without core-periphery structure.\nspectral_method: beta is the paper's lower bound b/n on the size of either side of the Find-Cut, not a prescribed core fraction. Sparse input uses ARPACK (tol = 1e-10, ncv = 48, maxiter = max(300, 20n)) and raises ConvergenceError when the two eigenpairs do not converge; dense input uses LAPACK.\nborgatti_everett_discrete: cp_block=:missing is the model Borgatti and Everett recommend and UCINET's categorical default; :ones remains the package default for continuity. method=:exhaustive returns a global optimum (first met in enumeration order among ties) and ignores n_runs, rng, init, and max_iter.\nContinuous detectors: core_nodes is the split produced by the partition keyword, not an estimate of the model. :median (default) puts nodes strictly above the median coreness in the core, so nodes tied at the median (a star's leaves, for instance) stay peripheral; :none returns an empty core; :threshold uses threshold (strictly above); :core_size takes the core_size highest scores, ties broken by lower node index. rombach_continuous additionally offers :beta, its default. Borgatti and Everett (2000) themselves do not partition in the continuous model, so report the scores rather than the split when the split is not an explicit analytic choice.\nIsolates and components: every detector except surprise_cp and spectral_method gives isolates (zero in- and out-strength) coreness 0 and never places them in core_nodes (multiple_cp_pairs gives them pair label 0); an edgeless network returns all-zero coreness and an empty core. The same detectors emit one warning per detector per session when the input has more than one connected component with at least two nodes; random_walker_profiling instead requires connected input.\n\nrossa_profile_ensemble is an uncertainty wrapper rather than another estimand. It summarizes repeated paper-mode Rossa profiles, centralization, rank stability, and raw replicates when requested. Its preflight memory_limit_bytes bounds the O(n*n_runs) profile workspace (512 MiB by default); store_replicates=false omits that workspace from the returned result after summaries are formed. Passing tie_break or rng through its forwarded keywords raises ArgumentError (the ensemble always uses :paper and seeds the profiles from its own rng). rossa_stationary_distribution is a numerical helper that returns the distribution, L1 balance residual, iteration count, and selected solver.\n\ncp_significance is also a wrapper. It compares one detector quality with a null distribution using a conservative plus-one Monte Carlo p-value over n_samples=999 samples by default, and reports null_mean, null_std, and zscore as effect sizes. Its supported nulls are undirected ER, undirected degree-preserving configuration, directed in/out-degree configuration, and topology-preserving weight permutation. The first three require a binary network; non-binary input raises an error naming :weight_permutation as the weighted alternative. Null samples are drawn in the storage format of the input (sparse input gives sparse samples) and dense and sparse inputs produce identical results for the same rng. With pass_rng=true (default) each detector call that accepts an rng keyword receives a child RNG derived from rng. With threaded=true the samples are distributed over a work queue with one task per thread (thread_schedule=:auto or :greedy) or round-robin (:static); every schedule is bit-for-bit identical to the serial run.\n\nThe switching null follows the prescribed-degree switching family discussed by Milo et al. (2003), including its important caveat that a finite switch chain has no general mixing-time guarantee. The weight-permutation null is the fixed-topology weight reshuffling design used to remove weight–topology association. It preserves the global edge-weight multiset, not individual node strengths; use it only when that conditional null is the scientific question.","title":"Detector contracts"},{"category":"section","location":"contracts/#Result-containers","page":"Contracts","text":"All result types (and CorePeriphery.NullDiagnostics) define structural ==, isequal, and hash over every field, and a compact show. == follows the field semantics, so a result whose quality or zscore is NaN is not == to itself; compare such results with isequal.","title":"Result containers"},{"category":"section","location":"contracts/#CPResult","page":"Contracts","text":"coreness is the method-specific node score described above. Isolates score 0.\ncore_nodes and periphery_nodes are the method-specific partition. They are not necessarily obtained by thresholding coreness; LowRank-Core is the key example, and for the continuous detectors they follow the partition keyword. Isolates are never in core_nodes.\nquality is method-specific and is only comparable within a common objective.\nalgorithm identifies the estimator.","title":"CPResult"},{"category":"section","location":"contracts/#CPDirectedResult","page":"Contracts","text":"out_coreness is the sender factor, in_coreness is the receiver factor, and coreness is their arithmetic mean after separate normalization. residual is the unscaled off-diagonal least-squares residual.","title":"CPDirectedResult"},{"category":"section","location":"contracts/#CPMultiResult","page":"Contracts","text":"In addition to pair labels and binary within-pair roles, this result records pair_selection, every evaluated candidate_pair_counts, raw candidate_qualities, and the selection_scores used to choose model complexity. Isolates carry pair label 0 and coreness 0; n_pairs counts the pairs among the non-isolated nodes, whose labels are 1:n_pairs.","title":"CPMultiResult"},{"category":"section","location":"contracts/#CPSignificanceResult","page":"Contracts","text":"pvalue is the plus-one Monte Carlo estimate (1 + #{null ≥ observed}) / (n_samples + 1). null_mean, null_std (sample standard deviation, NaN with a single sample), and zscore ((observed − null_mean) / null_std, NaN when the null is degenerate) are effect sizes to report alongside the p-value. null_diagnostics is a CorePeriphery.NullDiagnostics stating the constraint preserved by the selected null model. For switching nulls it also reports requested and accepted swaps, attempts, the attempt budget, and whether every sample completed. Incomplete switching raises ConvergenceError by default; swap_shortfall=:warn or :accept is an explicit opt-in.","title":"CPSignificanceResult"},{"category":"section","location":"contracts/#Directionality-guide","page":"Contracts","text":"Use directed Rossa when directionality means stationary flow and persistence inside a node subset. The graph must be strongly connected. Use minres_svd_directed when directionality means distinct sender and receiver roles in an asymmetric rank-one model. Use minres_symmetric for the symmetric w*w' estimand; do not compare it directly with the directed u*v' model as though they were the same optimizer.","title":"Directionality guide"},{"category":"section","location":"contracts/#Reproducible-examples","page":"Contracts","text":"julia> using CorePeriphery, Random, SparseArrays\n\njulia> A = adjacency_to_matrix([(1, 2), (1, 3), (1, 4), (2, 3)], 4);\n\njulia> dense = lip_discrete(A); sparse_result = lip_discrete(sparse(A));\n\njulia> @assert dense.coreness == sparse_result.coreness\n\njulia> paper = random_walker_profiling(A; tie_break=:paper, rng=MersenneTwister(7));\n\njulia> @assert isfinite(paper.quality)\n\njulia> using CorePeriphery\n\njulia> D = [0.0 2 0 1; 0 0 3 0; 4 0 0 1; 1 2 0 0];\n\njulia> stationary = rossa_stationary_distribution(D; method=:linear);\n\njulia> @assert stationary.residual <= 1e-12\n\njulia> directed = minres_svd_directed(D);\n\njulia> @assert length(directed.out_coreness) == 4","title":"Reproducible examples"},{"category":"section","location":"contracts/#Edge-cases-and-compatibility","page":"Contracts","text":"Input validation failures raise ArgumentError or DimensionMismatch. Numerical procedures that do not reach the requested accuracy raise ConvergenceError: an ARPACK eigensolve in sparse spectral_method, a Rossa stationary solver that exhausts its iteration budget or tolerance, and a configuration-null switching chain that cannot complete the requested swaps under swap_shortfall=:error.\nSymmetry is tested with an absolute tolerance of 1e-12 · max(1, max|A|), so floating-point noise such as (A + A') / 2 does not make an undirected network look directed. Symmetric, Hermitian, Transpose, and Adjoint wrappers around a sparse matrix are unwrapped to SparseMatrixCSC at entry so the sparse kernels are used.\nRossa cp-centralization is undefined for a two-node graph and is returned as NaN.\nRossa keywords n_walks and walk_length and Lip's max_iter are deprecated compatibility arguments: they do not alter the result and emit Base.depwarn (visible with --depwarn=yes).\nDeterministic Rossa final ties use node index and are therefore label-dependent. Use paper mode or the ensemble wrapper when tied order is scientifically meaningful.\nNumeric keywords accept any Real (tol=1//1000, alpha=1f-2) and ideal_cp_matrix accepts any real vector.\nadjacency_to_matrix rejects a dyad that occurs more than once with conflicting weights unless combine=:sum|:max|:min|:last is given; directed=true keeps (i, j) and (j, i) distinct. The method without n accepts arbitrary node identifiers and returns (A, labels).\nSparse storage preserves estimator semantics but is not guaranteed to reduce latency on small graphs.\nJulia 1.10 is the supported compatibility floor; Julia 1.13 is the primary development runtime and CI tests Julia 1.10, 1.12, and 1.13. The benchmark snapshots on this site were measured on Julia 1.10 and 1.12.","title":"Edge cases and compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"contracts/#Null-model-references","page":"Contracts","text":"Milo, R., Kashtan, N., Itzkovitz, S., Newman, M. E. J., and Alon, U. (2003). On the uniform generation of random graphs with prescribed degree sequences. arXiv:cond-mat/0312028.\nOpsahl, T., Colizza, V., Panzarasa, P., and Ramasco, J. J. (2008). Prominence and control: the weighted rich-club effect. Physical Review Letters 101, 168702.","title":"Null-model references"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Development-Record-and-Roadmap","page":"Development Record","text":"This page records the scientific audit, implementation refactor, performance work, and cross-package validation carried out in July 2026 for version 0.2.0, the expert-panel review of October 2026 and the revision it triggered, together with the limitations that remain. The work replaced the 0.1.0 implementation. The pre-refactor snapshot that the ablation and versioned benchmarks were measured against predates this repository's published history and cannot be checked out from it; see benchmark/README.md for what is and is not reproducible.","title":"Development Record and Roadmap"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Scope-and-evidence","page":"Development Record","text":"The work combined four perspectives: network-analysis definitions, Julia language and API design, software-engineering correctness, and high-performance computing. Published algorithm descriptions were checked against implementations, direct mathematical oracles were added where practical, and CorePeriphery.jl was compared empirically with Python cpnet commit 6aad458a6d434a3617d33e74f7163d514a27fecb.\n\nThe evidence is retained in four reports:\n\nbenchmark/COMPARISON.md records identical-data fits, recovery metrics, concordance, and warmed runtimes.\nbenchmark/ABLATION.md separates the pre-refactor implementation, current dense algorithms, and current sparse kernels.\nbenchmark/DIVERGENCE.md traces lower-concordance algorithms one mechanism at a time.\nbenchmark/OPTIMIZATION.md records before/after hotspot measurements and the current regression gate.","title":"Scope and evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Completed-scientific-and-API-work","page":"Development Record","text":"Area Completed work Resulting contract\nShared input validation Centralized square, finite, nonnegative, loop-free, symmetry, and binary checks, including sparse matrices Invalid inputs fail consistently instead of being silently reinterpreted\nBorgatti–Everett Reworked objective evaluation around sufficient statistics and protected degenerate inputs Reported quality is evaluated on the returned state; empty/constant cases are finite\nLip Replaced the previous heuristic with the published degree-prefix minimizer Simple undirected binary input; deterministic smallest minimizing prefix; reported quality is the discrete Pearson correlation of that prefix\nLowRank-Core Implemented rank-two reconstruction, thresholded row scores, and Find-Cut Binary undirected input; sparse matrices use two ARPACK eigenpairs without a dense reconstruction\nRombach Replaced coordinate-grid work with seeded template-permutation restarts and cached swap deltas; October 2026: exact eq. (4.9) template with ⌊βN⌋, sum-to-one normalisation, eq. (4.10) quality, partition=:beta, and the eq. (4.11) aggregate rombach_core_score Explicit RNG and reproducible restarts; beta is the periphery fraction; quality is the eq. (4.10) objective for one (alpha, beta) pair\nMINRES Added distinct symmetric ww' and directed uv' rank-one estimators Symmetric comparisons use minres_symmetric; CPDirectedResult retains sender, receiver, combined scores, residual, and explained-weight quality\nDella Rossa Implemented equation-(5) persistence and equation-(8) centralization, then added the published stochastic tie mode and directed stationary-flow formulation Deterministic compatibility default; tie_break=:paper with an RNG; directed input must be strongly connected\nSurprise Replaced the approximation with the multivariate-hypergeometric joint-tail statistic in log space Exact statistic for simple undirected binary graphs; greedy single-start optimiser\nMultiple CP pairs Added joint pair/role label switching plus compatibility and penalized pair-count selection; October 2026: restored the eq. (13) i = j null term, penalised selection by default, isolates labelled 0 Seeded restarts, pair-size constraints, and candidate objective/selection diagnostics in CPMultiResult; configuration objective is eq. (13) exactly; pair-count selection is a package variant\nSignificance Added ER, degree-switching, directed in/out-degree switching, and fixed-topology weight-permutation nulls; October 2026: effect sizes, n_samples=999, pass_rng=true, sparse-aware workspaces, work-queue scheduling Reproducible serial/threaded sampling, strict switch completion, and per-sample diagnostics through CPSignificanceResult\nBorgatti–Everett family (October 2026) Missing-block model (cp_block=:missing), directed correlation, restarts, Gray-code exhaustive optimum :ones and undirected remain the defaults; method=:exhaustive is a global optimum for n ≤ 24\nPartitions, isolates, components (October 2026) Shared _partition helper with strict median and explicit rules; _isolates/_enforce_isolates!; _warn_disconnected Ties at the median are periphery; isolates are never core; one warning per detector per session on disconnected input\nInput and reporting (October 2026) Labelled adjacency_to_matrix(edges), directed/combine keywords, block_densities Conflicting duplicate dyads raise unless combine= is given\nRossa robustness Added lazy, constrained-linear, and automatic stationary solvers plus paper-mode ensembles Solver residual/iteration metadata and serial/thread-reproducible uncertainty summaries\nEcosystem integration Added an optional Graphs.jl extension and generic dense/sparse matrix support Graph objects are converted lazily through the weak dependency; Graphs.jl is not a hard dependency\n\nLegacy n_walks and walk_length Rossa keywords remain accepted but are ignored (with a deprecation warning), because the implemented method computes persistence analytically rather than simulating walks. Julia 1.10 is the compatibility floor. The July 2026 measurements used Julia 1.10 and 1.12; Julia 1.13 is now the primary development runtime and CI tests all three.","title":"Completed scientific and API work"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#October-2026-panel-review-and-revision","page":"Development Record","text":"An expert panel (sociology, computational social science, SNA, network science, Julia, software engineering, science communication) reviewed the 0.2.0 working tree independently. Its consensus findings and the resulting changes:\n\nFinding Resolution\nThe >= median split made core_nodes meaningless for continuous detectors (stars all-core; Rombach beta did not change the core size) Strict > median; explicit partition keyword (:median, :none, :threshold, :core_size); Rombach partition=:beta derives the core from beta\nRombach beta documented backwards and the template used i/n instead of ⌊βN⌋; no aggregate core score Eq. (4.9) implemented exactly, beta is the periphery fraction, core vector sums to one, eq. (4.10) quality, rombach_core_score (eq. 4.11); tutorial grid search by quality replaced\nKM-configuration objective omitted the i = j term of eq. (13) (confirmed: 17% gap on the planted test) Term restored in _joint_expected and all move deltas; literal eq. (3)/(13) oracles; DIVERGENCE.md corrected\nBorgatti–Everett missing-block model and directed support absent cp_block=:missing, directed=true, n_runs, method=:exhaustive\nNo labelled input path; silent last-weight-wins symmetrisation adjacency_to_matrix(edges) returns (A, labels); directed/combine keywords; conflicting duplicates raise\nNull-model choice presented as interchangeable; no effect sizes; n_samples=199, pass_rng=false \"Which null answers which question\" guidance; null_mean/null_std/zscore; n_samples=999, pass_rng=true; weighted input with a binary null raises\nmultiple_cp_pairs over-partitioned with :objective and the ER null pair_selection=:penalized default; configuration null recommended in the docstring\nDisconnected components and isolates handled silently Isolate guarantee and one-time warning in every detector except Surprise and LowRank-Core\nThreaded :greedy significance intermittently non-deterministic Root cause (closure-captured buffer names shared across tasks) fixed; Channel work queue; 144,000-run stress with zero mismatches\nSurprise O(E²) per flip; ARPACK tol = 0 stalls; sparse wrappers fell to dense kernels; dense n × n null buffers per worker Mode-outward tail evaluation with rigorous truncation; finite ARPACK tolerance with ncv = 48; wrapper unwrapping; sparse _NullWorkspace\nCopyrighted PDFs in algo/; release provenance; missing hash; Float64-only keywords; monolithic source file Reference list by DOI; CI/CompatHelper/TagBot/Aqua; ==/isequal/hash/show on all results; Real keywords; one file per algorithm family\n\nDevelopment measurements for the performance items: Surprise n = 400, density 0.05, 15.1 s to 28 ms; planted n = 512, 11.2 s to 6 ms; sparse significance n = 2048, 1.0 s to 0.32 s; 2000-node ring LowRank-Core 13.4 s to about 3 s. On the revision host (Julia 1.13.2) the same cases measured 3.6 s to 24 ms, 0.32 s to 0.06 s, and 4.1 s to 1.2 s against the pre-revision commit.","title":"October 2026 panel review and revision"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Rossa-paper-audit","page":"Development Record","text":"The Della Rossa audit deserves separate treatment because it changed the interpretation of the earlier cross-package results.\n\nFor an undirected graph, CorePeriphery.jl incrementally evaluates the exact candidate persistence ratio. For a directed graph it computes the stationary distribution of the row-normalized transition matrix, then grows the peripheral set using stationary edge flow. Directed tie strength is total in-plus-out strength, as specified by the paper. A lazy stationary iteration preserves the target distribution and also converges on periodic strongly connected graphs.\n\nThe API distinguishes two selection policies:\n\ntie_break=:deterministic selects the lower node index after the paper's persistence and minimum-strength criteria. This preserves the package's reproducible default.\ntie_break=:paper randomly chooses the initial minimum-strength node and randomly resolves the paper's final ties. An explicit RNG makes a run repeatable.\n\nThe cpnet divergence is not just random tie variation. On noisy planted graphs, cpnet minimizes a non-equivalent surrogate and omits the secondary minimum-strength filter. It also reports 1 - mean(alpha) rather than equation-(8) centralization and does not implement the paper's stationary directed equation. The exact trace audit found a mean of 11.385 and 16.950 paper-rule violations per cpnet run on the noisy single-pair and two-pair graphs, respectively.","title":"Rossa paper audit"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Performance-refactor","page":"Development Record","text":"The main performance gains came from changing asymptotic work and data movement:\n\nsufficient statistics replaced repeated full Pearson-correlation scans;\ncached block counts replaced repeated Surprise rescans;\nincremental boundary weights replaced cubic Rossa set reconstruction;\nsparse LowRank-Core uses partial eigenpairs and streams rank-two scores;\nsymmetric and directed MINRES reuse matrix-vector workspaces and evaluate residuals analytically;\nmulti-pair moves use adjacency lists and cached pair aggregates;\nthreaded significance allocates deterministic child RNG streams and reuses per-worker or per-task buffers.\n\nDevelopment measurements reported approximately 339x and 336x improvements for one dense Borgatti–Everett discrete and continuous sweep, 32x for dense Rossa profiling, more than 1,000x for the exact Surprise workload, 33–72x for multi-pair workloads, 74x for sparse directed MINRES, and 17x for sparse LowRank-Core. These were same-machine, warmed development measurements against the pre-refactor snapshot, not portable performance guarantees.\n\nThe dense/sparse ablation is scientifically important: current dense and sparse paths produced identical top-k sets, explicit core sets, and pair partitions in all 27 fitted cases. Therefore storage-specific optimization does not explain the CorePeriphery.jl/ cpnet result divergence on this corpus. Sparse storage is a scalability path, not an automatic speedup for tiny graphs; it was faster in 11 of the 27 small-network fits.","title":"Performance refactor"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Cross-package-findings","page":"Development Record","text":"Across three planted networks and nine comparable algorithm configurations, CorePeriphery.jl was faster in 25 of 27 warmed rows, with a median cpnet/Julia runtime ratio of 33.6x in the checked comparison. The comparison reflects each package's configured estimator and restart policy, so it must not be interpreted as equal low-level work.\n\nLower concordance was traced to algorithmic or output-definition differences:\n\nThe headline MINRES row fits the same symmetric ww' estimand in both packages. cpnet still selects the largest residual across restarts, uses a scorer inconsistent with its gradient, and stops loosely. Correct selection and tighter convergence raise mean rank concordance with Julia from 0.320 to 0.918.\nMulti-pair methods enter different local basins and use different state constraints; cpnet additionally has inconsistent ER density conventions and a missing node-degree factor in the configuration move gain.\ncpnet Rombach exits after its first improving sweep. Completing the swap search recovers Julia's ordering on the noisy and two-pair cases.\nLowRank-Core exposes different node quantities: Julia retains reconstruction scores, while cpnet returns the binary Find-Cut state as coreness. The latent reconstruction rankings agree on all three graphs.\nRossa differs for the substantive reasons documented above.\n\nConcordance is diagnostic, not a claim that cpnet is ground truth. Several identically named methods expose different mathematical estimands.","title":"Cross-package findings"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Verification-completed","page":"Development Record","text":"As of the July 2026 audit, the 0.2.0 code base passed:\n\nthe full Pkg.test() suite on Julia 1.10 and 1.12 (about 1,500 checks at that time, including roughly 1,050 exhaustive/direct scientific-validation checks, sparse/dense equivalence, Graphs.jl dispatch, directed Rossa, statistical contracts, and threading);\na direct stationary linear-system oracle for weighted directed Rossa;\nseeded stochastic Rossa reproducibility and periodic directed-cycle tests;\nthe strict Documenter build and doctests;\ngit diff --check;\nthe cpnet divergence replay after the Rossa extension;\nthe dependency-free two-thread scaling and allocation gate on Julia 1.10 and 1.12.\n\nThe check counts are those recorded at the audit and change as the test suite evolves; the CI workflow is the authoritative record for the current tree. After the October 2026 revision the suite (about 546,000 checks, 541,852 of them from the brute-force Borgatti–Everett optimum over every graph with n ≤ 6) passes on Julia 1.10, 1.12, and 1.13.2 with two threads, together with Aqua.jl, the strict Documenter build on 1.13, examples/basic_usage.jl, git diff --check, and the quick scaling gate on 1.10 and 1.13.2.","title":"Verification completed"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Remaining-limitations-and-risks","page":"Development Record","text":"The empirical cross-package corpus contains only three small planted networks. The independent oracle suite is much broader at small n, but larger licensed real, temporal, weighted, directed, and adversarial corpora remain future research work.\nEdge switching has no universal finite mixing-time guarantee. CorePeriphery.jl guarantees that the requested accepted-swap count completes or is explicitly reported; that is a completion guarantee, not proof that a chain has mixed.\nThe direct stationary solver intentionally materializes dense matrices and is for smaller problems. Sparse :lazy remains subject to slow mixing, but failures report method, iterations, tolerance, and residual and users can supply a validated stationary vector.\nPair-count penalization controls the tested planted single-pair cases but is a model-selection heuristic, not a universal significance guarantee. Candidate scores are exposed so sensitivity to pair_penalty can be audited.\nThe weight-permutation null preserves topology and the global weight multiset, not node strengths. A strength-preserving weighted configuration null would answer a different question and remains a possible future feature.\nThe cross-package Python environment is manually assembled around the 2022 cpnet revision and NumPy 1.x; exact revisions are recorded, but this leg is not in CI.\nThe pre-refactor benchmark snapshot is not part of this repository's history, so the ablation's old-implementation leg and the versioned performance snapshot are historical records rather than reproducible measurements.","title":"Remaining limitations and risks"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Release-roadmap-resolution","page":"Development Record","text":"The audit roadmap was implemented in 0.2.0. Each tracking issue was closed after the corresponding change passed the supported-version CI and release checks.\n\nPriority Delivered change Tracking issue Status\n1 Explicit configuration-switch completion and diagnostics #3 Implemented and tested\n2 Alternative Rossa stationary solvers and convergence metadata #4 Implemented and tested\n3 Exhaustive/direct oracle and provenance validation matrix #5 Implemented and tested\n4 Penalized pair-count selection with candidate diagnostics #6 Implemented and tested\n5 Symmetric rank-one MINRES alongside directed MINRES #7 Implemented and tested\n6 Reproducible Rossa ensemble uncertainty summaries #8 Implemented and tested\n7 Directed configuration and weighted permutation significance #9 Implemented and tested\n8 Julia 1.10/1.12 versioned performance and allocation tracking #10 Implemented and tested\n9 Algorithm/result/directionality contract documentation #11 Implemented; strict docs pass\n10 Changelog, migration guide, version 0.2.0, and release review #12 Implemented","title":"Release-roadmap resolution"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Reproduction-commands","page":"Development Record","text":"From the repository root:\n\njulia +1.13.2 --threads=2 --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\njulia +1.13.2 --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia +1.13.2 --project=docs docs/make.jl\njulia +1.13.2 --threads=2 --project=. benchmark/scaling.jl --quick --check\n\nRepeat the tests on julia +1.10 and julia +1.12 before a release; the docs build needs Julia 1.11 or newer.\n\nThe full cross-package and ablation workflows, including pinned environment details and the parts that are no longer reproducible, are documented in benchmark/README.md.","title":"Reproduction commands"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Migrating-from-0.1-to-0.2","page":"Migration Guide","text":"Version 0.2 is a scientific and performance release, not a drop-in numerical patch. Review stored scores and partitions before replacing results generated by 0.1.","title":"Migrating from 0.1 to 0.2"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Julia-compatibility","page":"Migration Guide","text":"The supported floor is Julia 1.10. Julia 1.13 is the primary development runtime and CI also tests 1.12. Users on Julia 1.6–1.9 must upgrade Julia or remain on CorePeriphery.jl 0.1.","title":"Julia compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#0.2.0-default-changes","page":"Migration Guide","text":"These defaults changed during the 0.2.0 review. Each one alters results of code that relies on the previous behaviour, so check stored outputs against the new rule:\n\nStrict median partition. Continuous detectors (borgatti_everett_continuous, random_walker_profiling, minres_symmetric, minres_svd, minres_svd_directed, rombach_core_score) put only nodes strictly above the median coreness in core_nodes; nodes tied at the median are periphery (a star's leaves no longer join the hub, and a profile with many zero persistences no longer makes every node core). Choose the rule explicitly with partition=:none|:threshold|:core_size.\nRombach. beta is the periphery fraction of Rombach et al. (2017) eq. (4.9), implemented with the paper's ⌊βN⌋ boundary instead of the former i/n ranks; coreness is normalised to sum to one (it used to be the raw [0, 1] template); quality is the full double sum of eq. (4.10) (twice the former upper-triangular value on the same vector); and the default partition=:beta reports the N − ⌊βN⌋ core ranks as core_nodes instead of a median split. The new rombach_core_score provides the paper's aggregate core score (eq. 4.11).\nKojaku–Masuda configuration null. The :configuration objective of multiple_cp_pairs now includes the i = j null term of Kojaku & Masuda (2018) eq. (13), which the earlier implementation omitted; configuration-null qualities are lower by Σ_{i ∈ core} d_i² / (4m²) and fitted partitions can differ.\nPenalised pair selection. multiple_cp_pairs defaults to pair_selection=:penalized; pass pair_selection=:objective for the former raw-objective rule, which over-partitions a single planted pair.\nSignificance defaults. cp_significance draws n_samples=999 (was 199) and forwards a child RNG to detectors with an rng keyword (pass_rng=true, was false). The result gains null_mean, null_std, and zscore; use isequal to compare results whose z-score may be NaN.\nConflicting duplicate edges. adjacency_to_matrix raises an ArgumentError when a dyad occurs with conflicting weights; the previous last-weight-wins behaviour is available as combine=:last (also :sum, :max, :min), and directed=true keeps (i, j) and (j, i) distinct instead of symmetrising.\nIsolates and components. Isolates receive coreness 0 and are never core (multiple_cp_pairs gives them pair label 0); an edgeless network returns an empty core; detectors warn once per session on input with more than one non-trivial connected component.\nDeprecations. Rossa n_walks/walk_length and Lip max_iter emit Base.depwarn; rossa_profile_ensemble raises an error when tie_break or rng is passed through its forwarded keywords instead of silently overriding.\nErrors. Non-convergence (ARPACK, Rossa stationary solvers, incomplete configuration switching) raises the new ConvergenceError instead of ArgumentError/ErrorException; Symmetric/Transpose wrappers around sparse matrices are unwrapped so they take the sparse kernels.","title":"0.2.0 default changes"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Algorithm-output-changes","page":"Migration Guide","text":"Lip, LowRank-Core, Rombach, Rossa, Surprise, multi-pair detection, and several shared quality calculations now implement different or corrected published quantities. Do not expect historical scores to remain numerically identical. The ablation report separates semantic changes from storage-specific optimization.\n\nRaw quality values remain algorithm-specific. Replace code that selects an algorithm by maximum(result.quality) with a common external validation measure or a matched objective.","title":"Algorithm output changes"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Rossa","page":"Migration Guide","text":"Calls without new keywords retain deterministic node-index tie resolution:\n\nresult = random_walker_profiling(A)\n\nUse the paper's stochastic hierarchy explicitly:\n\nusing Random\nresult = random_walker_profiling(\n    A; tie_break=:paper, rng=MersenneTwister(42),\n)\n\nAsymmetric input is now accepted when strongly connected. The default directed solver remains sparse-friendly lazy iteration; stationary_method=:linear is available for a robust dense solve. n_walks and walk_length are ignored compatibility keywords and should be removed from new code.","title":"Rossa"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#MINRES","page":"Migration Guide","text":"minres_svd remains a CPResult compatibility projection of the directed u*v' model. Prefer explicit names in new code:\n\nminres_svd_directed for separate sender and receiver factors;\nminres_symmetric for the symmetric w*w' model.","title":"MINRES"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Multiple-pairs","page":"Migration Guide","text":"The pair count is now chosen by the BIC-inspired structural penalty by default; the 0.1-style raw-objective rule remains available:\n\nresult = multiple_cp_pairs(A; pair_selection=:objective)\n\nInspect candidate_pair_counts, candidate_qualities, and selection_scores rather than treating n_pairs as uncertainty-free. Kojaku & Masuda (2018) recommend the configuration null (multiple_cp_pairs_config, or null_model=:configuration), which is now their eq. (13) exactly; :er stays the default for continuity with the 2017 paper.","title":"Multiple pairs"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Borgatti-Everett-family","page":"Migration Guide","text":"borgatti_everett_discrete and label_switching_cp accept cp_block=:missing (the missing-block model of BE 2000 eq. 4), directed=true, and (discrete only) n_runs, rng, and method=:exhaustive; borgatti_everett_continuous accepts directed=true. Defaults are unchanged (cp_block=:ones, undirected, one greedy start), so existing calls return the same fits apart from the strict median split and the isolate rule.","title":"Borgatti-Everett family"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Significance-testing","page":"Migration Guide","text":"Degree-preserving switching must now complete all requested swaps. Networks with no valid simple switch raise a ConvergenceError instead of silently returning an ineffective null. Use a larger max_swap_attempts, select a different null model, or explicitly accept the limitation:\n\nresult = cp_significance(\n    A, detector; swap_shortfall=:accept,\n)\n@assert !result.null_diagnostics.complete\n\nNew :directed_configuration and :weight_permutation nulls support directed binary and weighted workflows. Their preserved constraints are recorded in null_diagnostics. The binary nulls reject weighted input with an error that points to :weight_permutation. Sparse input is now sampled in sparse form; dense and sparse inputs give identical results for the same rng.","title":"Significance testing"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Graphs.jl-and-sparse-input","page":"Migration Guide","text":"Graphs.jl is now a weak dependency. Loading both packages activates direct detector methods for graph objects. Sparse matrices preserve estimator definitions, but small graphs may remain faster as dense matrices.","title":"Graphs.jl and sparse input"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Result-structures","page":"Migration Guide","text":"Code using named fields remains the recommended pattern. Positional construction or destructuring of CPMultiResult and CPSignificanceResult must account for their new diagnostic and effect-size fields. All result types now define ==, isequal, and hash, so they can be deduplicated and used as dictionary keys; null_diagnostics is the concrete CorePeriphery.NullDiagnostics type.\n\nSee the algorithm contract page, development record, and changelog for the complete behavioral inventory.","title":"Result structures"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Performance","page":"Performance","text":"CorePeriphery.jl has separate dense and sparse execution paths. Use a SparseMatrixCSC when the network is sparse; validation, Pearson quality, LowRank-Core, directed MINRES, random-walker profiling, and multi-pair fitting then operate on stored edges or sparse matrix-vector products instead of scanning all possible dyads.\n\nFor a directed Rossa profile, the stationary distribution is computed by a lazy matrix-vector iteration that remains sparse and converges even for periodic strongly connected graphs. stationary_tol trades precision for runtime; increase stationary_max_iter only when a slowly mixing network does not reach the requested tolerance, or use stationary_method=:linear for a dense constrained solve.\n\nusing CorePeriphery, SparseArrays\n\nA = sparse(adjacency_to_matrix([(1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 3), (1, 4)], 4))\nresult = random_walker_profiling(A)","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Parallel-significance-tests","page":"Performance","text":"Monte Carlo samples can run concurrently. Start Julia with multiple threads and set threaded=true:\n\nresult = cp_significance(\n    A,\n    lip_discrete;\n    null_model=:er,\n    n_samples=1_000,\n    threaded=true,\n)\n\nThe package assigns a deterministic child RNG to each sample before scheduling, so a fixed input RNG produces the same sample sequence in serial and threaded runs. The default thread_schedule=:auto (and :greedy) feeds the samples through a work queue with one task per thread, which balances irregular null-model and detector runtimes; :static assigns samples round-robin to one task per thread and cannot be used from inside another threaded region. Every schedule gives the same result bit for bit on every supported Julia version. Null samples are drawn in the storage format of the input, so pass a SparseMatrixCSC for large sparse networks: the samplers assemble each null directly in compressed sparse column form and the detectors use their sparse kernels. When nesting threaded significance tests around algorithms that use BLAS, set BLAS to one thread to avoid oversubscription:\n\nusing LinearAlgebra\nBLAS.set_num_threads(1)","title":"Parallel significance tests"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Cross-package-benchmark","page":"Performance","text":"CorePeriphery.jl was compared with Python cpnet commit 6aad458a6d434a3617d33e74f7163d514a27fecb on identical sparse matrices. The comparison covers every algorithm configuration with a counterpart in both packages, including discrete Borgatti–Everett (BE). The three fixtures are small planted correctness cases:\n\nDataset Nodes Undirected edges Density\nideal_single 20 85 44.7%\nnoisy_single 40 162 20.8%\ntwo_pairs 48 124 11.0%\n\nEach package was warmed once, after which the reported runtime was the median of five fits. Numba compilation was excluded, RNG state was reset before each cpnet fit, both packages received sparse matrices, and BLAS was restricted to one thread. In the table, each fixture cell is CorePeriphery.jl ms / cpnet ms (cpnet/Julia ratio); a ratio above 1 means that the Julia call completed sooner.\n\nAlgorithm Fit budget (Julia / cpnet) ideal_single noisy_single two_pairs Median ratio Julia faster\nBE 1 deterministic fit / 5 starts 0.004 / 0.139 (36.2×) 0.008 / 1.172 (141.8×) 0.008 / 1.539 (204.0×) 141.8× 3/3\nLip 1 deterministic fit / 1 deterministic fit 0.004 / 0.066 (17.7×) 0.006 / 0.058 (10.3×) 0.005 / 0.049 (9.5×) 10.3× 3/3\nLowRank-Core 1 deterministic fit / 1 deterministic fit 0.080 / 2.690 (33.6×) 0.343 / 3.458 (10.1×) 0.216 / 4.311 (20.0×) 20.0× 3/3\nRombach 5 starts / 5 starts 0.064 / 0.646 (10.0×) 2.378 / 1.341 (0.6×) 3.295 / 2.247 (0.7×) 0.7× 1/3\nRossa 1 fit / 1 fit 0.004 / 1.600 (416.1×) 0.015 / 2.495 (171.7×) 0.017 / 3.783 (222.6×) 222.6× 3/3\nMINRES 1 deterministic fit / 5 starts 0.007 / 4.160 (623.8×) 0.022 / 4.273 (196.8×) 0.113 / 5.699 (50.5×) 196.8× 3/3\nSurprise 1 deterministic fit / 5 starts 0.007 / 12.910 (1834.7×) 0.932 / 48.760 (52.3×) 0.285 / 43.590 (152.9×) 152.9× 3/3\nKM-ER 5 starts / 5 starts 0.069 / 1.039 (15.1×) 0.180 / 4.236 (23.6×) 0.178 / 4.850 (27.2×) 23.6× 3/3\nKM-configuration 5 starts / 5 starts 0.066 / 1.313 (19.9×) 0.234 / 5.127 (21.9×) 0.155 / 5.342 (34.4×) 21.9× 3/3\n\nAcross these 27 fitted calls, CorePeriphery.jl was faster in 25 and the median of the 27 per-fixture cpnet/Julia ratios was 33.6×. Rombach was the exception: cpnet was faster on the two larger fixtures. This is a comparison of each package's configured estimator, not equal optimizer work. In particular, cpnet uses five starts for BE, MINRES, and Surprise where CorePeriphery.jl uses a deterministic fit. The largest ratios are therefore not per-restart speedups.\n\nThe comparison also has three important limits:\n\nthese n = 20--48 measurements are descriptive microbenchmarks, and the many sub-millisecond Julia entries are sensitive to timer noise and host load;\nthe median ratio in each algorithm row is the median of three paired ratios, not a ratio of runtimes pooled across differently sized fixtures;\nidentically named methods can expose different objectives or node scores. The MINRES row deliberately uses minres_symmetric in Julia to match cpnet's symmetric off-diagonal ww' model; it does not benchmark minres_svd_directed.\n\nSee the COMPARISON.md report for recovery and concordance metrics, environment versions, and the complete 27-row result table. The raw fitted outputs and reproduction scripts are retained in the repository's benchmark/ directory.","title":"Cross-package benchmark"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Julia-scaling-and-regression-benchmark","page":"Performance","text":"The independent Julia harness separates dense and sparse scaling, warms compilation before timing, records allocations, and pins BLAS to one thread. Run it from the repository root as described in benchmark/README.md.\n\nFor reliable measurements, use a fresh Julia process, report allocations as well as elapsed time, and compare scaling over several network sizes and densities. GPU execution is not currently exposed: most greedy algorithms are sequential, while the matrix-free sparse CPU paths avoid transfer and launch overhead that would dominate typical network sizes.\n\nCorePeriphery.jl supports Julia 1.10 and newer. Julia 1.13 is the primary development and documentation runtime; Julia 1.10 is the compatibility floor. The regression gate compares the minimum over repetitions (5 in --quick mode, 7 in full mode) against the targets in benchmark/regression_targets.jl and reports minimum, median, and maximum columns. Run benchmark/run_versioned_performance.sh to regenerate the Julia 1.10/1.12/1.13 TSV and Markdown snapshot. The committed VERSIONED_PERFORMANCE.md report is a historical Julia 1.10/1.12 snapshot (it predates the Surprise and sparse significance rewrites, so its Surprise rows are obsolete) with seven-repetition ranges through n = 512, source/CPU/thread/BLAS provenance, allocations, dense/sparse LowRank-Core, Surprise, directed Rossa, symmetric and directed MINRES, penalized multi-pair fitting, and significance scheduling.","title":"Julia scaling and regression benchmark"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Algorithms","page":"Algorithms","text":"CorePeriphery.jl implements ten published core-periphery detection methods (the MINRES method has distinct symmetric and directed estimators) plus Monte Carlo significance testing. This page provides detailed descriptions of each method, their theoretical foundations, and guidance on when to use them. Full citations are in the References section at the end of the page.","title":"Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Algorithm-Categories","page":"Algorithms","text":"The algorithms can be grouped into three categories:\n\nContinuous methods: Assign each node a coreness score in [0, 1]\nDiscrete methods: Classify nodes as either core (1) or periphery (0)\nMulti-pair methods: Detect multiple non-overlapping core-periphery pairs","title":"Algorithm Categories"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Borgatti-Everett-Continuous-Model","page":"Algorithms","text":"result = borgatti_everett_continuous(A; max_iter=1000, tol=1e-6, step=0.05, init=nothing,\n                                     directed=false, partition=:median)\n\nThe classic continuous core-periphery model introduced by Borgatti and Everett (2000). It finds a coreness vector c that maximizes the correlation between the adjacency matrix A and an ideal core-periphery pattern:\n\nDelta_ij = c_i times c_j\n\nAlgorithm: Bounded grid coordinate ascent on the stated Pearson objective. Only objective-improving updates are accepted.\n\nParameters:\n\nmax_iter: Maximum iterations (default: 1000)\ntol: Convergence tolerance (default: 1e-6)\nstep: Coordinate-search resolution (default: 0.05)\ninit: Initial coreness vector (default: degree centrality)\ndirected: Accept asymmetric input and correlate over all ordered off-diagonal dyads, as UCINET does for directed sociometric data (default: false)\npartition, threshold, core_size: Split rule for core_nodes: :median (default, strictly above the median), :none, :threshold, or :core_size\n\nBorgatti and Everett do not partition in the continuous model; core_nodes is a reporting convenience. Report the scores, the fit, and the block densities (block_densities) rather than the split alone.\n\nWhen to use: When you need continuous coreness scores and want a well-established baseline method.","title":"Borgatti-Everett Continuous Model"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Borgatti-Everett-Discrete-Model","page":"Algorithms","text":"result = borgatti_everett_discrete(A; max_iter=1000, init=nothing, directed=false,\n                                   cp_block=:ones, n_runs=1, rng=Random.default_rng(),\n                                   method=:greedy)\n\nThe discrete version of the Borgatti-Everett model. Finds a binary partition maximizing correlation with the ideal discrete pattern. With cp_block=:ones (default) the pattern is their equation (2),\n\nDelta_ij = max(c_i c_j)\n\nwhere c_i in 0 1, so core-periphery ties are expected to be present. With cp_block=:missing the pattern is their equation (4), the model Borgatti and Everett recommend and UCINET's categorical default: core-periphery dyads are treated as missing data and only the core-core (pattern 1) and periphery-periphery (pattern 0) dyads enter the correlation.\n\nAlgorithm: Greedy single-node flipping from a degree-based start (method=:greedy), optionally with n_runs random restarts, or Gray-code enumeration of every core set (method=:exhaustive, n ≤ 24), which returns a global optimum. Greedy flipping misses the optimum on a few percent of small graphs, so prefer :exhaustive for small sociograms.\n\nParameters:\n\nmax_iter: Maximum sweeps per start (default: 1000)\ninit: Initial binary assignment (default: degree-based)\ndirected: Accept asymmetric input and correlate over all ordered off-diagonal dyads\ncp_block: :ones (equation 2) or :missing (equation 4)\nn_runs, rng: Random restarts and their generator (n_runs=1 is deterministic)\nmethod: :greedy or :exhaustive\n\nWhen to use: When you need a binary classification and want to maximize correlation with the ideal discrete pattern.","title":"Borgatti-Everett Discrete Model"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Lip's-Fast-Discrete-Algorithm","page":"Algorithms","text":"result = lip_discrete(A)\n\nThe exact binary algorithm introduced by Lip (2011). It minimizes missing core-core edges plus observed periphery-periphery edges over degree-sorted prefix partitions.\n\nAlgorithm: Stable degree sort followed by a linear scan of all nontrivial prefix sizes. Input must be simple, undirected, and binary.\n\nQuality: The returned quality is the discrete Borgatti-Everett Pearson correlation of the returned partition. The Lip loss is minimised internally but is not returned, so quality can be negative even though the loss is at its minimum (two disjoint cliques are an example).\n\nWhen to use: When you need an exact, fast discrete classification of a simple binary graph. Lip minimises an error count rather than the correlation, so its partition can differ from borgatti_everett_discrete and label_switching_cp, which maximise the correlation directly.","title":"Lip's Fast Discrete Algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Rombach's-Generalized-Model","page":"Algorithms","text":"result = rombach_continuous(A; alpha=0.5, beta=0.5, n_runs=10, rng=Random.default_rng(),\n                            partition=:beta)\nscore = rombach_core_score(A; alphas=0.1:0.1:1.0, betas=0.1:0.1:1.0, n_runs=3)\n\nA generalized continuous model by Rombach et al. (2017) with parameters controlling the core-periphery structure:\n\nalpha: The size of the score jump between the highest-scoring peripheral node and the lowest-scoring core node (alpha = 1 is a discrete classification)\nbeta: The fraction of nodes in the periphery (paper semantics): the first lfloor beta N rfloor template ranks are peripheral, so the core shrinks from N nodes at beta = 0 to none at beta = 1\n\nThe parameters define the transition-function template of the paper's equation (4.9), implemented exactly with the lfloor beta N rfloor boundary and normalised so the core vector sums to one, as in the paper. The algorithm assigns a permutation of that template to nodes.\n\nAlgorithm: Seedable random restarts with objective-improving pair swaps, maximizing the equation-(4.10) core quality R_alphabeta = sum_ij A_ij C_i C_j (the full double sum, so each undirected edge counts twice).\n\nThe returned coreness is the optimised normalised template for one (alpha beta) pair, and the default partition=:beta reports the N - lfloor beta N rfloor nodes holding the template's core ranks as core_nodes, so beta sets the core size directly. The quality is R_alphabeta of the returned vector; it depends on the template as well as on the data, so it must not be used to select (alpha beta). The paper's recommended deliverable is the aggregate core score of equation (4.11), CS(i) = Z sum_gamma C_i(gamma) R_gamma over a grid of gamma = (alpha beta) values, which rombach_core_score provides (the paper's 100 times 100 grid is reduced to 10 times 10 by default; refine with alphas/betas).\n\nParameters:\n\nalpha: Boundary sharpness, 0 to 1 (default: 0.5)\nbeta: Periphery fraction, 0 to 1 (default: 0.5); larger beta, smaller core\nn_runs: Number of random restarts (default: 10; 3 per grid point in rombach_core_score)\npartition, threshold, core_size: Split rule; :beta (default), :median, :none, :threshold, or :core_size (rombach_core_score defaults to :median)\n\nWhen to use: When you want to tune the expected core-periphery structure, explore how results change with different boundary definitions, or report the parameter-averaged core score.","title":"Rombach's Generalized Model"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Spectral-Method","page":"Algorithms","text":"result = spectral_method(A)\n\nImplements Cucuringu et al.'s LowRank-Core algorithm: reconstruct the adjacency matrix from its two eigenpairs of largest magnitude, threshold the rank-two matrix, rank nodes by its row sums, and apply Find-Cut.\n\nAlgorithm: Rank-two symmetric eigendecomposition plus the published core/core-periphery/periphery density cut objective. The returned CPResult contains the LowRank-Core row-score coreness values, while core_nodes and periphery_nodes come from the best Find-Cut prefix rather than a threshold on those scores. Input is binary, undirected, and loop-free.\n\nParameters: beta is the paper's lower bound bn on the fraction of nodes allowed on either side of the Find-Cut (default 0.1), not the prescribed core fraction beta of the paper's known-core-size variant, which is not implemented.\n\nConvergence: Dense input uses a full LAPACK eigendecomposition. Sparse input computes only the two largest-magnitude eigenpairs with ARPACK (tol = 1e-10, ncv = 48 Krylov vectors, maxiter = max(300, 20n) restarts; calls are serialised through a lock because ARPACK is not reentrant). If fewer than two eigenpairs converge, a ConvergenceError reports the settings; clustered extremal eigenvalues (a long ring, for instance) are the usual cause, and a dense matrix is the fallback. The thresholded reconstruction is pruned by a per-row bound, so only rows that can exceed the 12 threshold are paired; on sparse core-periphery networks that is a small fraction of n, but the worst case stays quadratic in the number of active rows.\n\nWhen to use: Fast method for continuous coreness scores. Works well when the core-periphery structure aligns with the network's spectral properties. No greedy optimization is needed; the sparse path is an iterative eigensolve.","title":"Spectral Method"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Random-Walker-Profiling","page":"Algorithms","text":"result = random_walker_profiling(A)\n\nImplements Della Rossa et al.'s persistence profile. Starting from a minimum-strength node, it grows a nested peripheral set by selecting the node that minimizes the new set's persistence probability.\n\nAlgorithm: Greedy persistence profiling for undirected and directed networks. The default tie_break=:deterministic replaces the paper's random choices with lower node index while retaining its persistence and secondary strength criteria. Use tie_break=:paper and an explicit rng to sample exactly according to the published tie hierarchy. quality is the paper's equation (8) cp-centralization.\n\nInput must be connected when undirected, strongly connected when directed, loop-free, and nonnegative. Directed inputs use total in-plus-out strength for the tie hierarchy and the stationary distribution of the row-normalized random walk for persistence. stationary_tol and stationary_max_iter control its sparse-friendly lazy iteration. Use stationary_method=:linear for a robust dense constrained solve or :auto to select by storage and size. rossa_stationary_distribution exposes convergence residual and iteration metadata, while rossa_profile_ensemble summarizes uncertainty across paper-mode tie realizations.\n\nWhen to use: When you want a dynamics-based definition of coreness. Useful for understanding network flow and accessibility.","title":"Random Walker Profiling"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#MINRES/SVD-Method","page":"Algorithms","text":"result = minres_svd(A; max_iter=1000, tol=1e-6)\n\nMinimizes the residual between the adjacency matrix and an outer product model (Boyd et al., 2010):\n\nmin_uv sum_i neq j (A_ij - u_i v_j)^2\n\nThis yields separate in-coreness (v) and out-coreness (u) vectors. minres_svd_directed retains both, their combined scores, the residual, and the explained squared-weight quality. minres_svd returns a combined compatibility result.\n\nAlgorithm: Alternating least squares optimization.\n\nParameters:\n\nmax_iter: Maximum iterations (default: 1000)\ntol: Convergence tolerance (default: 1e-6)\npartition, threshold, core_size: Split rule for core_nodes (default :median)\n\nWhen to use: For directed (asymmetric) outer-product structure. The current CPResult reports the average of in- and out-coreness; directed quality uses all ordered off-diagonal dyads.\n\nFor a symmetric rank-one model, use:\n\nresult = minres_symmetric(A; max_iter=10_000, tol=1e-8, damping=0.5, partition=:median)\n\nThis fits A_ijapprox w_iw_j and is a different estimand from the directed u_iv_j model.","title":"MINRES/SVD Method"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Multiple-Core-Periphery-Pairs","page":"Algorithms","text":"result = multiple_cp_pairs(A; max_pairs=10, min_pair_size=2, max_iter=100,\n                           null_model=:er, pair_selection=:penalized)\n\nDetects multiple non-overlapping core-periphery pairs using the Q^cp quality function (Kojaku & Masuda, 2017, 2018).\n\nAlgorithm:\n\nCreate balanced seeded initial pair assignments\nJointly propose each node as core or periphery in its own or a neighboring pair\nAccept only objective-improving moves across multiple restarts\n\nReturns a CPMultiResult with pair labels, within-pair coreness, the chosen pair count, and the candidate qualities inspected during model selection. Isolates are removed before fitting and reported with pair label 0.\n\nObjectives: Under null_model=:er the objective is Kojaku & Masuda (2017) equation (3) divided by m. Under :configuration it is Kojaku & Masuda (2018) equation (13) exactly, including the paper's i = j null term -d_i^2  (2m) for every core node (so the objective reduces to modularity when every node is core, as the paper states); both are checked against literal transcriptions of the equations in the test suite. Earlier development versions omitted the i = j term, which lowered the configuration objective by sum_i in textcore d_i^2  (4m^2).\n\nPackage variants: The objectives and move gains follow the papers, but the search and model-selection procedures are package variants rather than the published heuristics. The optimiser seeds balanced initial assignments for each candidate pair count from 1 to max_pairs instead of growing pairs from singleton cores, and the pair count is chosen either by the raw objective (:objective) or by an unpublished penalty pair_penalty * (k - 1) * log(n) / n (:penalized, the default, because the raw objective is nondecreasing in the pair count in practice and over-partitions a single planted pair). The per-pair statistical test of Kojaku & Masuda (2018), which discards pairs that are not significant under the configuration model, is not implemented. Kojaku & Masuda (2018) recommend the configuration null because the ER null finds core-periphery pairs in any network with heterogeneous degrees; :er remains the default for continuity with the 2017 paper, and multiple_cp_pairs_config selects the configuration null directly.\n\nParameters:\n\nmax_pairs: Maximum number of pairs (default: 10)\nmin_pair_size: Minimum nodes per pair (default: 2)\nmax_iter: Optimization iterations (default: 100)\nn_runs: Seeded restarts (default: 10)\nnull_model: :er (default) or :configuration (the only nulls accepted here; :directed_configuration and :weight_permutation exist only in cp_significance)\nrng: Explicit random-number generator\npair_selection: :penalized (default) for complexity control or :objective for the raw objective\npair_penalty: Multiplier for (k-1)log(n)n (default: 0.5)\n\nWhen to use: When you expect the network to contain multiple distinct core-periphery structures rather than a single global one.\n\nUse multiple_cp_pairs_config(A) as a convenience wrapper for the degree-preserving configuration null.","title":"Multiple Core-Periphery Pairs"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Significance-Testing","page":"Algorithms","text":"result = cp_significance(A, lip_discrete; null_model=:configuration, n_samples=999)\n\nCompares one detector's observed quality against a Monte Carlo null distribution of n_samples (default 999) random networks. The wrapper accepts any detector that returns a finite quality field and supports four null models:\n\n:er for undirected Erdős-Rényi sampling\n:configuration for undirected degree-preserving edge switching\n:directed_configuration for directed in/out-degree-preserving arc switching\n:weight_permutation for topology-preserving weight shuffling\n\nThe first three preserve properties of a simple graph and therefore require binary input; a weighted network raises an error that names :weight_permutation as the alternative. The result reports the plus-one p-value and the effect sizes null_mean, null_std, and zscore.\n\npass_rng=true (default) forwards a fresh child RNG, derived from rng, to every detector call whose method accepts an rng keyword, so stochastic detectors give reproducible null distributions; detectors defined outside the package that lack an rng keyword trigger a one-time warning. Null samples are drawn in the storage format of A, so a sparse input is sampled directly into sparse form and evaluated with the sparse kernels; dense and sparse inputs give identical results. With threaded=true, the null samples are distributed across threads from a precomputed seed stream: thread_schedule=:auto (the default) and :greedy use a work queue with one task per thread, :static assigns samples round-robin, and every schedule reproduces the serial result bit for bit on every supported Julia version.\n\nConfiguration-style nulls require the requested swap budget to complete by default; a shortfall raises ConvergenceError with an explanation (stars and other threshold graphs admit no double-edge swap at all). Use max_swap_attempts to raise the proposal budget, or set swap_shortfall=:warn or :accept if an incomplete chain is scientifically acceptable and you want that fact recorded in null_diagnostics.","title":"Significance Testing"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Which-null-model-answers-which-question","page":"Algorithms","text":"The null model decides what \"significant\" means, and the choice can flip the answer:\n\n:er preserves only the edge count. A small p-value says the network is more core-periphery-like than a random graph of the same density.\n:configuration preserves every node's degree. A small p-value says the core-periphery signal goes beyond degree heterogeneity. A few high-degree nodes attached to many low-degree nodes already produce a high core-periphery quality under :er; the configuration null removes that explanation (Kojaku & Masuda, 2018).\n:directed_configuration preserves in- and out-degrees but not reciprocity.\n:weight_permutation preserves the topology and the multiset of weights, not node strengths; it asks whether the weights rather than the edges carry the structure.\n\nA network with a planted core can be significant under :er and not under :configuration. That is the expected outcome when the planted structure is mostly a degree effect, not a contradiction. Report both p-values and name the null model behind each one. The Tutorial contains a worked example. The analytic inference framework of Yanchenko, Sengupta & Mukherjee (2025, arXiv:2508.04730) is not implemented.","title":"Which null model answers which question"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Surprise-Based-Detection","page":"Algorithms","text":"result = surprise_cp(A; max_iter=100)\n\nUses statistical surprise to find partitions that are unlikely under a random null model (Jeude et al., 2019). Surprise is higher when:\n\nCore-core and core-periphery edges are more than expected\nPeriphery-periphery edges are fewer than expected\n\nAlgorithm: Greedy single-start node flipping from a mean-degree initial split, accepting objective-improving moves. The statistic is the paper's exact joint-tail probability, but this optimiser is a package variant; the paper's search procedure is not reproduced. The tail sum is evaluated from the mode of the hypergeometric terms outwards with constant-time ratio updates and truncated once the geometrically bounded remainder is below double-precision resolution (checked against a BigInt oracle for small graphs), so one evaluation costs roughly the product of the widths of the two hypergeometric peaks rather than the full O(m^2) double sum. A greedy sweep is therefore about n such evaluations plus the neighbour counts of each node (O(n) dense, O(textdegree) sparse); a 400-node Erdős-Rényi graph at density 0.05 now fits in tens of milliseconds instead of seconds.\n\nParameters:\n\nmax_iter: Maximum iterations (default: 100)\n\nWhen to use: When you want a statistically-motivated definition of core-periphery structure.","title":"Surprise-Based Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Label-Switching-Algorithm","page":"Algorithms","text":"result = label_switching_cp(A; max_iter=100, n_runs=1, directed=false, cp_block=:ones)\n\nA seedable greedy algorithm that optimizes the discrete Borgatti-Everett Pearson correlation while excluding degenerate all-core/all-periphery states. It optimises the same objective as borgatti_everett_discrete (including the directed and cp_block variants) and is the stronger optimiser of the two because the random sweep order and restarts escape more local optima.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\nInitialize based on node degrees\nProcess nodes in random order\nFlip labels that improve the objective\nRepeat from multiple seeded starts when requested\n\nParameters:\n\nmax_iter: Maximum iterations (default: 100)\nn_runs: Number of starts (default: 1)\nrng: Explicit random-number generator\ndirected: Accept asymmetric input and correlate over all ordered off-diagonal dyads\ncp_block: :ones (Borgatti-Everett equation 2) or :missing (equation 4)\n\nWhen to use: Fast discrete classification, especially for large networks. Similar to Lip's algorithm but with different optimization strategy.","title":"Label Switching Algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Disconnected-input-and-isolates","page":"Algorithms","text":"Core-periphery objectives are defined for a single connected network. On a union of components the optimiser may assemble a core from several components or label a whole component as periphery, so the package makes two guarantees and one check:\n\nIsolates are never core. borgatti_everett_continuous, borgatti_everett_discrete, label_switching_cp, lip_discrete, rombach_continuous, rombach_core_score, minres_symmetric, minres_svd, minres_svd_directed, and multiple_cp_pairs give every node with zero in- and out-strength coreness 0 and keep it out of core_nodes (multiple_cp_pairs reports pair label 0). Rombach and the multi-pair detector drop isolates before fitting, so the fit equals that of the non-isolated subnetwork; the Borgatti-Everett correlations keep isolates as peripheral dyads of the sociogram. An edgeless network returns all-zero coreness and an empty core.\nDisconnected input warns. The same detectors emit a warning, once per detector per Julia session (maxlog=1), when the input has more than one connected component with at least two nodes. Isolates alone do not trigger it. The result is still returned; analyse each component separately when the components are substantively separate networks. The check is skipped for the null samples drawn inside cp_significance, which are random graphs and often disconnected; the observed network is still checked.\nRossa requires connectivity. random_walker_profiling and rossa_stationary_distribution raise an ArgumentError unless undirected input is connected or directed input is strongly connected, because the persistence profile is undefined otherwise.\n\nsurprise_cp and spectral_method apply neither the isolate rule nor the warning: Surprise scores the partition of the whole simple graph, and LowRank-Core's Find-Cut is defined on the full score order. Inspect coreness for isolates before reporting their partitions on fragmented input.","title":"Disconnected input and isolates"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Comparison-Summary","page":"Algorithms","text":"The table lists one row per public estimator; minres_svd is the CPResult projection of minres_svd_directed, and multiple_cp_pairs_config is the configuration-null specialization of multiple_cp_pairs.\n\nAlgorithm Type Speed Directed Key Feature\nborgatti_everett_continuous Continuous Medium With directed=true Classic baseline\nborgatti_everett_discrete Discrete Medium (exhaustive: exponential) With directed=true Discrete correlation; missing-block variant; exact option\nlip_discrete Discrete Fast No Exact degree-prefix optimum\nrombach_continuous Continuous Slow No Tunable template; beta sets the core size\nrombach_core_score Continuous Slow (grid of fits) No Parameter-averaged aggregate core score\nspectral_method Continuous Fast No No greedy iteration; sparse eigensolve\nrandom_walker_profiling Continuous Medium Yes Stationary-flow persistence\nminres_symmetric Continuous Medium No Symmetric rank-one residual\nminres_svd Continuous Medium Yes Handles asymmetric networks\nmultiple_cp_pairs Multi-pair Medium No Multiple structures\nsurprise_cp Discrete Medium No Exact surprise statistic\nlabel_switching_cp Discrete Fast With directed=true Efficient label updates","title":"Comparison Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#algorithm-references","page":"Algorithms","text":"Borgatti, S.P., Everett, M.G. (2000). Models of core/periphery structures. Social Networks, 21(4), 375-395. doi:10.1016/S0378-8733(99)00019-2\nLip, S.Z.W. (2011). A fast algorithm for the discrete core/periphery bipartitioning problem. arXiv:1102.5511. doi:10.48550/arXiv.1102.5511\nRombach, M.P., Porter, M.A., Fowler, J.H., Mucha, P.J. (2017). Core-periphery structure in networks (revisited). SIAM Review, 59(3), 619-646. doi:10.1137/17M1130046\nCucuringu, M., Rombach, P., Lee, S.H., Porter, M.A. (2016). Detection of core-periphery structure in networks using spectral methods and geodesic paths. European Journal of Applied Mathematics, 27(6), 846-887. doi:10.1017/S095679251600022X\nDella Rossa, F., Dercole, F., Piccardi, C. (2013). Profiling core-periphery network structure by random walkers. Scientific Reports, 3, 1467. doi:10.1038/srep01467\nBoyd, J.P., Fitzgerald, W.J., Mahutga, M.C., Smith, D.A. (2010). Computing continuous core/periphery structures for social relations data with MINRES/SVD. Social Networks, 32(2), 125-137. doi:10.1016/j.socnet.2009.09.003\nKojaku, S., Masuda, N. (2017). Finding multiple core-periphery pairs in networks. Physical Review E, 96(5), 052313. doi:10.1103/PhysRevE.96.052313\nKojaku, S., Masuda, N. (2018). Core-periphery structure requires something else in the network. New Journal of Physics, 20, 043012. doi:10.1088/1367-2630/aab547\nvan Lidth de Jeude, J., Caldarelli, G., Squartini, T. (2019). Detecting core-periphery structures by surprise. EPL (Europhysics Letters), 125(6), 68001. doi:10.1209/0295-5075/125/68001\nYanchenko, E., Sengupta, S. (2025). A label-switching algorithm for fast core-periphery identification. arXiv:2506.02069. doi:10.48550/arXiv.2506.02069\nYanchenko, E., Sengupta, S., Mukherjee, D. (2025). Statistical inference for core-periphery structures. arXiv:2508.04730. Related work; not implemented.\nMilo, R., Kashtan, N., Itzkovitz, S., Newman, M.E.J., Alon, U. (2003). On the uniform generation of random graphs with prescribed degree sequences. arXiv:cond-mat/0312028. Null-model reference for the switching chains.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Tutorial","page":"Tutorial","text":"This tutorial walks through common use cases for CorePeriphery.jl.","title":"Tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Creating-Networks","page":"Tutorial","text":"","title":"Creating Networks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#From-Edge-Lists","page":"Tutorial","text":"The most common way to create a network is from an edge list:\n\nusing CorePeriphery\n\n# Unweighted edges as (source, target) tuples\nedges = [\n    (1, 2), (1, 3), (1, 4),\n    (2, 3), (2, 4),\n    (3, 4),\n    (5, 1), (6, 2)  # Peripheral nodes\n]\nA = adjacency_to_matrix(edges, 6)","title":"From Edge Lists"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Weighted-Networks","page":"Tutorial","text":"For weighted networks, include weights as the third element:\n\nweighted_edges = [\n    (1, 2, 5.0),  # Strong connection\n    (1, 3, 3.0),\n    (2, 3, 4.0),\n    (4, 1, 1.0),  # Weak peripheral connection\n]\nA = adjacency_to_matrix(weighted_edges, 4)\n\nA dyad listed twice with the same weight is stored once; a dyad listed with conflicting weights raises an ArgumentError unless you say how to resolve it (combine=:sum, :max, :min, or :last). Pass directed=true when (i, j) and (j, i) are distinct arcs rather than the same undirected dyad.","title":"Weighted Networks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Labelled-Node-Identifiers","page":"Tutorial","text":"Relational data rarely arrives with nodes numbered 1:n. Omit n and the edge list may use any identifiers; the call returns the matrix together with the label of every row/column, in order of first appearance (or sorted with sort_labels=true):\n\njulia> using CorePeriphery\n\njulia> friendships = [(\"alice\", \"bob\"), (\"alice\", \"carol\"), (\"bob\", \"carol\"),\n                      (\"alice\", \"dave\"), (\"bob\", \"erin\"), (\"carol\", \"frank\")];\n\njulia> A, labels = adjacency_to_matrix(friendships);\n\njulia> labels\n6-element Vector{String}:\n \"alice\"\n \"bob\"\n \"carol\"\n \"dave\"\n \"erin\"\n \"frank\"\n\njulia> result = lip_discrete(A);\n\njulia> labels[result.core_nodes]\n3-element Vector{String}:\n \"alice\"\n \"bob\"\n \"carol\"\n\nIndex labels with core_nodes, periphery_nodes, or sortperm(result.coreness) to report results in the original identifiers.","title":"Labelled Node Identifiers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#From-Existing-Matrices","page":"Tutorial","text":"If you already have an adjacency matrix, you can use it directly:\n\nusing LinearAlgebra\n\n# Create a 6x6 adjacency matrix\nA = zeros(6, 6)\nA[1, 2] = A[2, 1] = 1.0\nA[1, 3] = A[3, 1] = 1.0\n# ... etc","title":"From Existing Matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Basic-Core-Periphery-Detection","page":"Tutorial","text":"","title":"Basic Core-Periphery Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Running-an-Algorithm","page":"Tutorial","text":"All algorithms follow the same pattern:\n\nresult = borgatti_everett_continuous(A)","title":"Running an Algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Understanding-Results","page":"Tutorial","text":"The result is a CPResult struct with several fields:\n\n# Coreness scores (0 to 1, higher = more core-like)\nprintln(result.coreness)\n\n# Nodes classified as core\nprintln(result.core_nodes)\n\n# Nodes classified as periphery\nprintln(result.periphery_nodes)\n\n# Algorithm-specific quality score\nprintln(result.quality)\n\n# Algorithm name\nprintln(result.algorithm)\n\nFor the continuous detectors core_nodes is not part of the model: it is the split chosen by the partition keyword. The default :median puts the nodes strictly above the median coreness in the core (ties at the median stay peripheral, so a star's leaves never join its hub). Make the rule an explicit analytic choice when you report it:\n\nborgatti_everett_continuous(A; partition=:none)                      # scores only\nborgatti_everett_continuous(A; partition=:threshold, threshold=0.5)  # strictly above 0.5\nborgatti_everett_continuous(A; partition=:core_size, core_size=3)    # three highest\n\nIsolates (nodes with no ties) always receive coreness 0 and are never core, and a network with more than one non-trivial connected component triggers a one-time warning: core-periphery objectives assume one connected network.","title":"Understanding Results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Reporting-a-Fit","page":"Tutorial","text":"The standard companion of a Borgatti-Everett fit is the density table of the four blocks induced by the partition (UCINET's density table). block_densities computes it from any result with core_nodes (or from an explicit core set):\n\njulia> using CorePeriphery\n\njulia> A = adjacency_to_matrix([(1, 2), (1, 3), (1, 4), (1, 5), (2, 3), (2, 4), (2, 5),\n                                (3, 4), (3, 5), (6, 1), (7, 2)], 7);\n\njulia> result = borgatti_everett_continuous(A);\n\njulia> result.core_nodes\n3-element Vector{Int64}:\n 1\n 2\n 3\n\njulia> block_densities(A, result)\n(core_core = 1.0, core_periphery = 0.6666666666666666, periphery_core = 0.6666666666666666, periphery_periphery = 0.0)\n\nReport the fit (quality), the core size, the block densities, and a significance test (below) together; a correlation of 0.6 with a dense periphery block means something different from the same correlation with an empty one.","title":"Reporting a Fit"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Visualizing-Results","page":"Tutorial","text":"# Sort nodes by coreness\nsorted_idx = sortperm(result.coreness, rev=true)\n\nprintln(\"Node ranking by coreness:\")\nfor (rank, idx) in enumerate(sorted_idx)\n    score = round(result.coreness[idx], digits=3)\n    label = idx in result.core_nodes ? \"CORE\" : \"PERIPHERY\"\n    println(\"  $rank. Node $idx: $score ($label)\")\nend","title":"Visualizing Results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Comparing-Algorithms","page":"Tutorial","text":"Different algorithms may identify different core-periphery structures:\n\n# Run multiple algorithms\nresults = [\n    borgatti_everett_continuous(A),\n    borgatti_everett_discrete(A),\n    lip_discrete(A),\n    spectral_method(A),\n    random_walker_profiling(A),\n    minres_svd(A),\n    surprise_cp(A),\n    label_switching_cp(A)\n]\n\n# Display quality scores. Their scales differ by method, so do not rank\n# algorithms by these raw values.\nprintln(\"Algorithm Comparison:\")\nprintln(\"-\" ^ 50)\nfor r in results\n    q = round(r.quality, digits=4)\n    n_core = length(r.core_nodes)\n    println(\"$(rpad(r.algorithm, 30)) Quality: $q  Core: $n_core\")\nend","title":"Comparing Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Measuring-Agreement","page":"Tutorial","text":"To compare which nodes different algorithms classify as core:\n\njulia> function jaccard_similarity(set1, set2)\n           intersection_size = length(intersect(Set(set1), Set(set2)))\n           union_size = length(union(Set(set1), Set(set2)))\n           return union_size > 0 ? intersection_size / union_size : 1.0\n       end;\n\njulia> jaccard_similarity([1, 2, 3], [2, 3, 4])\n0.5\n\nApply it to the core sets returned by two detectors:\n\nr1 = borgatti_everett_continuous(A)\nr2 = spectral_method(A)\n\nsimilarity = jaccard_similarity(r1.core_nodes, r2.core_nodes)\nprintln(\"Jaccard similarity of core sets: $similarity\")","title":"Measuring Agreement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Working-with-Specific-Algorithms","page":"Tutorial","text":"","title":"Working with Specific Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Tuning-Rombach's-Model","page":"Tutorial","text":"The Rombach model has two template parameters: alpha sets the sharpness of the core-periphery boundary and beta is the fraction of nodes in the periphery (paper semantics): the first ⌊βN⌋ ranks of the template are peripheral and the remaining N − ⌊βN⌋ are core, so a smaller beta yields a larger core. The default partition=:beta reports exactly those N − ⌊βN⌋ nodes as core_nodes, so beta is the core size you ask for, and coreness is the optimised template normalised to sum to one:\n\nusing Random\n\n# Sharp boundary, large core (30% of nodes peripheral)\nresult1 = rombach_continuous(A; alpha=0.9, beta=0.3)\n\n# Soft boundary, small core (70% of nodes peripheral)\nresult2 = rombach_continuous(A; alpha=0.1, beta=0.7)\n\nDo not select (alpha, beta) by maximising result.quality. The quality is the equation-(4.10) objective of one template; it depends on the template as well as on the data, and the paper never selects a single (alpha, beta) by it. Treat the parameters as analyst choices and report a sensitivity sweep of the resulting core sets:\n\nfor beta in (0.2, 0.5, 0.8)\n    r = rombach_continuous(A; alpha=0.5, beta=beta, n_runs=5, rng=MersenneTwister(1))\n    println(\"beta = $beta: core_nodes = $(r.core_nodes)\")\nend\n\nRombach et al.'s own deliverable is the aggregate core score of their equation (4.11): the template-optimal vectors averaged over a grid of (alpha, beta) values and weighted by their objective, scaled so the largest score is one. rombach_core_score computes it (a 10 × 10 grid by default; the paper uses 100 × 100):\n\nscore = rombach_core_score(A; alphas=0.1:0.1:1.0, betas=0.1:0.1:1.0, n_runs=3,\n                           rng=MersenneTwister(1))\nscore.coreness   # aggregate core score, maximum 1\nscore.core_nodes # strict-median split of the score (configurable via `partition`)\n\nCompare the core sets across settings (for example with jaccard_similarity above) or validate them against an external criterion.","title":"Tuning Rombach's Model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Random-Walker-Requirements","page":"Tutorial","text":"random_walker_profiling accepts connected undirected or strongly connected directed, loop-free networks with nonnegative weights. It uses lower node index for a reproducible profile by default. To reproduce the original paper's stochastic selection hierarchy, supply an explicit RNG:\n\nusing Random\npaper_result = random_walker_profiling(\n    A; tie_break=:paper, rng=MersenneTwister(42),\n)\n\nDirected inputs use total in-plus-out strength for ties and a lazy stationary iteration for the row-normalized random walk. Its convergence controls are stationary_tol and stationary_max_iter. The legacy n_walks and walk_length keywords remain accepted for compatibility but do not affect the result.\n\nFor slowly mixing directed graphs, inspect the solver directly or select the robust dense solve:\n\nD = [0.0 2 0 1; 0 0 3 0; 4 0 0 1; 1 2 0 0]\nstationary = rossa_stationary_distribution(D; method=:linear)\nresult = random_walker_profiling(D; stationary_method=:linear)\n\nWhen paper-mode ties make node ranks unstable, summarize an ensemble:\n\nensemble = rossa_profile_ensemble(\n    A; n_runs=200, rng=MersenneTwister(42), threaded=true,\n)\nensemble.rank_stability","title":"Random-Walker Requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Directed-Networks-with-MINRES/SVD","page":"Tutorial","text":"For separate sender and receiver roles rather than a stationary-flow profile, use minres_svd_directed:\n\n# Create a directed network (asymmetric matrix)\nA_directed = zeros(5, 5)\nA_directed[1, 2] = 1.0  # 1 → 2\nA_directed[1, 3] = 1.0  # 1 → 3\nA_directed[2, 3] = 1.0  # 2 → 3\nA_directed[4, 1] = 1.0  # 4 → 1\nA_directed[5, 2] = 1.0  # 5 → 2\n\nresult = minres_svd_directed(A_directed)\n\nresult.out_coreness and result.in_coreness retain the two directed roles; result.coreness is their combined ranking.","title":"Directed Networks with MINRES/SVD"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Multiple-Core-Periphery-Pairs","page":"Tutorial","text":"Some networks contain multiple distinct CP structures:\n\n# Create a network with two communities, each with CP structure\nA = zeros(12, 12)\n\n# Community 1 (nodes 1-6): nodes 1-2 are core\nfor i in 1:2, j in 1:6\n    if i != j\n        A[i, j] = A[j, i] = 1.0\n    end\nend\n\n# Community 2 (nodes 7-12): nodes 7-8 are core\nfor i in 7:8, j in 7:12\n    if i != j\n        A[i, j] = A[j, i] = 1.0\n    end\nend\n\n# Detect multiple pairs\nresult = multiple_cp_pairs(A)\n\nprintln(\"Number of CP pairs detected: $(result.n_pairs)\")\nprintln(\"Pair labels: $(result.pair_labels)\")\nprintln(\"Within-pair coreness: $(result.coreness)\")\n\nThe two communities above are disconnected from each other, so this call also emits the one-time disconnected-input warning; that is expected for a planted two-pair fixture.\n\nPrefer multiple_cp_pairs_config(A), or multiple_cp_pairs(A; null_model=:configuration), for the degree-preserving configuration null (Kojaku & Masuda 2018 equation 13): under the default ER null any network with heterogeneous degrees exhibits core-periphery pairs, because high-degree nodes look \"core\" relative to a uniform density.\n\nThe number of pairs is chosen with pair_selection=:penalized by default, which discourages unsupported extra pairs; the raw-objective rule (:objective) tends to over-partition a single planted pair. Inspect candidate_pair_counts, candidate_qualities, and selection_scores in the result rather than treating n_pairs as uncertainty-free. Isolates are reported with pair label 0.","title":"Multiple Core-Periphery Pairs"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Statistical-Significance","page":"Tutorial","text":"using Random\n\ntest_result = cp_significance(\n    A,\n    lip_discrete;\n    null_model=:configuration,\n    n_samples=999,            # the default; 199 is enough for a quick look\n    rng=MersenneTwister(42),\n)\nprintln(test_result.pvalue)      # plus-one Monte Carlo p-value\nprintln(test_result.zscore)      # effect size: (observed - null mean) / null sd\nprintln(test_result.null_mean, \" ± \", test_result.null_std)\nprintln(test_result.null_diagnostics.preserved)  # constraint preserved by the null\nprintln(test_result.null_diagnostics.complete)   # did every switching chain complete?\n\nReport the p-value together with the effect size and the null model: a tiny p-value with a z-score of 1.5 on a large network says something different from a z-score of\n\nThe ER, configuration, and directed-configuration nulls preserve properties of a\n\nsimple graph and therefore require binary input; a weighted network raises an error pointing to :weight_permutation.\n\nAdditional nulls support directed binary and weighted networks:\n\nweighted_A = adjacency_to_matrix(\n    [(1, 2, 0.5), (1, 3, 1.5), (2, 3, 2.5), (2, 4, 3.5)], 4,\n)\n\ndirected_test = cp_significance(\n    A_directed, minres_svd_directed;\n    null_model=:directed_configuration,\n    n_samples=199,\n)\n\nweighted_test = cp_significance(\n    weighted_A, borgatti_everett_continuous;\n    null_model=:weight_permutation,\n    n_samples=199,\n)\n\nConfiguration switching must complete by default. The returned null_diagnostics records the preserved constraint, requested and accepted swaps, attempts, and the attempt budget; accepting a shortfall requires an explicit swap_shortfall=:warn or :accept.","title":"Statistical Significance"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Which-null-model-answers-which-question","page":"Tutorial","text":"The null model is not an implementation detail; it decides what \"significant\" means.\n\n:er keeps only the number of edges. A small p-value says the network is more core-periphery-like than a random graph of the same density.\n:configuration keeps every node's degree. A small p-value says the core-periphery signal goes beyond degree heterogeneity. A few high-degree nodes attached to many low-degree nodes already produce a high core-periphery quality under :er; the configuration null removes that explanation.\n:directed_configuration is the directed analogue (in- and out-degrees preserved, reciprocity not preserved).\n:weight_permutation keeps the topology and shuffles the weights; it asks whether the weights, not the edges, carry the structure.\n\nA network with a planted core can therefore be significant under :er and not under :configuration:\n\nA_planted = generate_cp_network(12, 48; p_cc=0.8, p_cp=0.3, p_pp=0.05, seed=1)\ner_test = cp_significance(A_planted, lip_discrete; null_model=:er,\n                          n_samples=199, rng=MersenneTwister(42))\ncm_test = cp_significance(A_planted, lip_discrete; null_model=:configuration,\n                          n_samples=199, rng=MersenneTwister(42))\nprintln(\"ER p = $(er_test.pvalue); configuration p = $(cm_test.pvalue)\")\n\n(generate_cp_network is defined in the synthetic-network section below.) A disagreement between the two is the expected outcome when the planted structure is mostly a degree effect, not a contradiction. Report both p-values and state which null model each one uses.\n\nStochastic detectors (label_switching_cp, rombach_continuous, borgatti_everett_discrete with restarts, ...) receive a fresh child RNG derived from rng on every call because pass_rng=true is the default, so the whole test is reproducible from rng alone. When you run many samples, threaded=true distributes them across threads from a precomputed seed stream; serial and threaded runs give the same result bit for bit. Sparse input is sampled directly into sparse form, which is the fast path for large networks.","title":"Which null model answers which question"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Quality-Assessment","page":"Tutorial","text":"","title":"Quality Assessment"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Computing-Quality-Manually","page":"Tutorial","text":"# Get a coreness vector (from algorithm or custom)\nc = result.coreness\n\n# Compute quality with continuous ideal pattern\nq_cont = core_quality(A, c; discrete=false)\n\n# Compute quality with discrete ideal pattern (strict median split, as in the\n# package's default partition rule for continuous methods)\nusing Statistics\nc_binary = Float64.(c .> median(c))\nq_disc = core_quality(A, c_binary; discrete=true)\n\nprintln(\"Continuous quality: $q_cont\")\nprintln(\"Discrete quality: $q_disc\")","title":"Computing Quality Manually"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Ideal-Pattern-Visualization","page":"Tutorial","text":"# Generate the ideal CP matrix\nc = [1.0, 0.9, 0.8, 0.3, 0.2, 0.1]  # Example coreness\nideal_cont = ideal_cp_matrix(c; discrete=false)\nideal_disc = ideal_cp_matrix(c; discrete=true)\n\n# Compare with actual adjacency\nprintln(\"Continuous ideal pattern:\")\ndisplay(round.(ideal_cont, digits=2))\n\nprintln(\"\\nDiscrete ideal pattern:\")\ndisplay(round.(ideal_disc, digits=2))","title":"Ideal Pattern Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Synthetic-Network-Generation","page":"Tutorial","text":"For testing and validation, create networks with known CP structure:\n\nusing Random\n\nfunction generate_cp_network(n_core, n_periphery;\n                             p_cc=0.8, p_cp=0.4, p_pp=0.05, seed=1)\n    rng = MersenneTwister(seed)   # explicit RNG: no global seeding\n    n = n_core + n_periphery\n    A = zeros(Float64, n, n)\n\n    for i in 1:n, j in (i+1):n\n        # Determine probability based on node types\n        if i <= n_core && j <= n_core\n            p = p_cc  # Core-core\n        elseif i <= n_core || j <= n_core\n            p = p_cp  # Core-periphery\n        else\n            p = p_pp  # Periphery-periphery\n        end\n\n        if rand(rng) < p\n            A[i, j] = A[j, i] = 1.0\n        end\n    end\n\n    return A\nend\n\n# Generate and test\nA = generate_cp_network(5, 15; seed=42)\nresult = borgatti_everett_continuous(A)\n\n# True labels (first 5 are core); `core_nodes` is the strict-median split\ntrue_core = Set(1:5)\ndetected_core = Set(result.core_nodes)\n\nprecision = length(intersect(true_core, detected_core)) / length(detected_core)\nrecall = length(intersect(true_core, detected_core)) / length(true_core)\n\nprintln(\"Precision: $(round(precision, digits=3))\")\nprintln(\"Recall: $(round(recall, digits=3))\")\n\nA sparse random periphery can leave a peripheral node without ties, or split off a small component; the detectors then give that node coreness 0 and, for a second non-trivial component, warn once. Check sum(A, dims=2) before interpreting such a fixture.","title":"Synthetic Network Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"#CorePeriphery.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia package for detecting core-periphery structure in networks.","title":"CorePeriphery.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Home","text":"Core-periphery structure is a fundamental mesoscale pattern in complex networks where nodes are partitioned into two groups:\n\nCore: A densely interconnected group of central nodes\nPeriphery: Sparsely connected nodes that attach to the core but not to each other\n\nThis pattern appears in many real-world networks including social networks, economic systems, biological networks, and transportation infrastructure. CorePeriphery.jl provides multiple algorithms for detecting and quantifying this structure.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"Published discrete, continuous, spectral, directed, and multi-pair methods, including the Borgatti-Everett missing-block model, an exact discrete optimum for small sociograms, and Rombach's aggregate core score\nWeighted network support for analyzing networks with edge weights\nDirected in/out coreness via CPDirectedResult, and directed=true in the Borgatti-Everett family\nLabelled edge lists (adjacency_to_matrix(edges) returns the matrix and node labels) and block_densities for reporting a fit\nOptional Graphs.jl integration and sparse-matrix input\nMonte Carlo significance tests with undirected, directed, and weighted nulls, effect sizes (zscore), sparse-aware sampling, and explicit swap-completion diagnostics\nMatrix-free sparse kernels for spectral, directed, and greedy methods\nConsistent API with unified result structures across all algorithms, explicit partition rules for continuous scores, and an isolate guarantee (isolates are never core)","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"CorePeriphery.jl is not yet in the General registry. Install it from GitHub:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/simoneSantoni/CorePeriphery.jl\")\n\nOr for development:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.develop(path=\"/path/to/CorePeriphery.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"julia> using CorePeriphery\n\njulia> edges = [\n           (1, 2), (1, 3), (1, 4), (1, 5),  # Node 1 connected to many\n           (2, 3), (2, 4), (2, 5),           # Node 2 also well-connected\n           (3, 4), (3, 5),\n           (6, 1), (7, 2)                    # Peripheral nodes\n       ];\n\njulia> A = adjacency_to_matrix(edges, 7);\n\njulia> result = borgatti_everett_continuous(A)\nCPResult: Borgatti-Everett Continuous\n  nodes = 7, core = 3, periphery = 4\n  quality = 0.7818\n\njulia> println(\"Coreness scores: \", result.coreness)\nCoreness scores: [0.7, 0.7, 0.65, 0.5, 0.5, 0.2, 0.2]\n\njulia> println(\"Core nodes: \", result.core_nodes)\nCore nodes: [1, 2, 3]\n\njulia> println(\"Periphery nodes: \", result.periphery_nodes)\nPeriphery nodes: [4, 5, 6, 7]\n\njulia> println(\"Quality: \", result.quality)\nQuality: 0.78178482001133\n\njulia> block_densities(A, result)   # report the block densities with the fit\n(core_core = 1.0, core_periphery = 0.6666666666666666, periphery_core = 0.6666666666666666, periphery_periphery = 0.0)\n\nNode identifiers need not be integers: A, labels = adjacency_to_matrix(edges) accepts any identifiers and returns the label of every row, so labels[result.core_nodes] reports the core in the original names (see the Tutorial).","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Choosing-an-Algorithm","page":"Home","text":"Use Case Recommended Algorithm\nFast binary classification lip_discrete or label_switching_cp\nExact discrete optimum on a small sociogram borgatti_everett_discrete with method=:exhaustive\nContinuous coreness scores borgatti_everett_continuous or rombach_continuous\nParameter-averaged Rombach core score rombach_core_score\nPublished spectral partition spectral_method\nDirected networks random_walker_profiling, minres_svd_directed, or the Borgatti-Everett family with directed=true\nSymmetric MINRES minres_symmetric\nMultiple CP structures multiple_cp_pairs\nTunable core size rombach_continuous (beta is the periphery fraction)\nStatistical significance and effect sizes cp_significance\nConfiguration null convenience multiple_cp_pairs_config\nBlock-density reporting block_densities\n\nRaw quality values are algorithm-specific; do not use them to rank algorithms against each other.","title":"Choosing an Algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"#Performance-snapshots","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Performance snapshots"},{"category":"section","location":"#CorePeriphery.jl-compared-with-cpnet","page":"Home","text":"Across three identical planted fixtures and all nine comparable algorithm configurations, CorePeriphery.jl was faster in 25 of 27 warmed fits. The table reports the median cpnet/Julia runtime ratio across the three fixtures; a value above 1 means that the Julia call completed sooner. Fit budgets are not matched: cpnet runs five stochastic starts for BE, MINRES, and Surprise where the Julia call is a single deterministic fit, so the largest ratios compare configured estimators rather than equal optimizer work.\n\nAlgorithm Median cpnet/Julia ratio Julia faster\nBorgatti–Everett discrete (BE, borgatti_everett_discrete) 141.8× 3/3\nLip (lip_discrete) 10.3× 3/3\nLowRank-Core (spectral_method) 20.0× 3/3\nRombach (rombach_continuous) 0.7× 1/3\nRossa (random_walker_profiling) 222.6× 3/3\nsymmetric MINRES (minres_symmetric) 196.8× 3/3\nSurprise (surprise_cp) 152.9× 3/3\nKM-ER (multiple_cp_pairs, null_model=:er) 23.6× 3/3\nKM-configuration (multiple_cp_pairs_config) 21.9× 3/3\n\nThese are configured-estimator timings on small n = 20, 40, 48 fixtures, not equal-work or large-network speedups. Several cpnet configurations use more stochastic starts than their deterministic Julia counterparts. See Performance for every timing pair, fit budgets, fixture densities, methodology, and interpretation limits.","title":"CorePeriphery.jl compared with cpnet"},{"category":"section","location":"#Julia-dense-and-sparse-scaling","page":"Home","text":"CorePeriphery.jl provides matrix-free sparse paths for its principal spectral, directed, and greedy algorithms. On the package's synthetic n = 512 scaling fixture, the following warmed median runtimes were measured with Julia 1.12, one BLAS thread, and seven repetitions:\n\nAlgorithm Dense Sparse Sparse speedup\nlip_discrete 0.906 ms 0.132 ms 6.8×\nminres_symmetric (max_iter = 50) 1.565 ms 0.337 ms 4.6×\nspectral_method 39.754 ms 4.215 ms 9.4×\nrandom_walker_profiling 18.480 ms 0.832 ms 22.2×\nminres_svd_directed (max_iter = 50) 7.096 ms 0.675 ms 10.5×\n\nAbsolute timings are host-specific. The scaling results show the effect of sparse storage on the benchmark fixture and are not intended to predict runtime for every network.","title":"Julia dense and sparse scaling"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\"tutorial.md\", \"algorithms.md\", \"contracts.md\", \"performance.md\", \"development.md\", \"migration.md\", \"api.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#References","page":"Home","text":"Full citations with DOIs for every implemented method are listed on the Algorithms page.\n\nBorgatti, S.P., Everett, M.G. (2000). Models of core/periphery structures. Social Networks, 21(4), 375-395. doi:10.1016/S0378-8733(99)00019-2\nLip, S.Z.W. (2011). A fast algorithm for the discrete core/periphery bipartitioning problem. arXiv:1102.5511.\nRombach, M.P., Porter, M.A., Fowler, J.H., Mucha, P.J. (2017). Core-periphery structure in networks (revisited). SIAM Review, 59(3), 619-646. doi:10.1137/17M1130046\nCucuringu, M., Rombach, P., Lee, S.H., Porter, M.A. (2016). Detection of core-periphery structure in networks using spectral methods and geodesic paths. European Journal of Applied Mathematics, 27(6), 846-887. doi:10.1017/S095679251600022X\nDella Rossa, F., Dercole, F., Piccardi, C. (2013). Profiling core-periphery network structure by random walkers. Scientific Reports, 3, 1467. doi:10.1038/srep01467\nBoyd, J.P., Fitzgerald, W.J., Mahutga, M.C., Smith, D.A. (2010). Computing continuous core/periphery structures for social relations data with MINRES/SVD. Social Networks, 32(2), 125-137. doi:10.1016/j.socnet.2009.09.003\nKojaku, S., Masuda, N. (2017). Finding multiple core-periphery pairs in networks. Physical Review E, 96(5), 052313. doi:10.1103/PhysRevE.96.052313\nKojaku, S., Masuda, N. (2018). Core-periphery structure requires something else in the network. New Journal of Physics, 20, 043012. doi:10.1088/1367-2630/aab547\nvan Lidth de Jeude, J., Caldarelli, G., Squartini, T. (2019). Detecting core-periphery structures by surprise. EPL (Europhysics Letters), 125(6), 68001. doi:10.1209/0295-5075/125/68001\nYanchenko, E., Sengupta, S. (2025). A label-switching algorithm for fast core-periphery identification. arXiv:2506.02069.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Home","text":"@software{SantoniCorePeripheryJL,\n  author = {Santoni, Simone},\n  title = {CorePeriphery.jl: Core-Periphery Detection in Julia},\n  version = {0.2.0},\n  year = {2026},\n  url = {https://github.com/simoneSantoni/CorePeriphery.jl},\n  note = {Julia package, version 0.2.0. Source code and documentation at the URL above.}\n}\n\nA machine-readable CITATION.cff is included in the repository.","title":"Citation"}]
